WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.320
- Hello, and I'm happy to
welcome you to the second

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:19.140
of this year's Franke
Lectures in the Humanities

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.550
here at the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:25.270
Each year, the annual Franke
Lectures in the Humanities

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.750
are organized in conjunction
with an upper level

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.663
undergraduate humanities seminar.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.880
The lectures are made
possible by the generosity

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.880
of Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.010
and are intended to
present important topics

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.340
in the humanities to a
wide and general audience.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.660
The Fall 2021 Franke
Lectures in the Humanities

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.470
has been organized in conjunction

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.810
with the Yale College
seminar, Mass Incarceration

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.930
in the Soviet Union and the United States.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:57.650
And it's a tremendous honor
to present today's speaker.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:01.920
Khalil Gibran Muhammad is
the Ford Foundation Professor

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.800
of History, Race and Public
Policy at the Harvard

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.810
Kennedy School and Director
of the Institutional

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.143
Anti-racism and Accountability Project.

00:01:11.022 --> 00:01:13.570
He is the former director
of the Schomburg Center

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.960
for Research in Black Culture, a division

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.370
of the New York Public
Library, and the world's

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:21.853
leading library and archive
of global black history.

00:01:23.297 --> 00:01:25.710
He is the author of the award-winning

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:30.041
Condemnation of Blackness: Race, Crime,

00:01:30.041 --> 00:01:33.223
and the Making of Modern Urban America,

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.850
and a contributor to a 2014
National Research Council study,

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.590
The Growth of Incarceration
in the United States:

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.690
Exploring Causes and Consequences.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.670
He is currently co-chairing
a National Academies

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:48.797
of Sciences Report on
reducing racial inequalities

00:01:48.797 --> 00:01:51.050
in the criminal justice system.

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:54.320
And I know that that it's not an award,

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.520
but I regularly describe this book

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.823
as one of the most important
books in the 21st century.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:05.020
It's about the United States,
but it is I think maybe

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.300
the best book, certainly
one of the best books

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:12.300
ever written, on the ideological
misuse of objectivity.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.500
Khalil is a frequent
reviewer and commentator

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:21.010
in national print and
broadcast media outlets,

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.530
such as the Washington Post, the Nation,

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.920
National Public Radio, PBS NewsHour,

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:29.300
MSNBC and the New York
times, which includes

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.050
his sugar essay for the 1619 Project.

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:34.470
Also a remarkable piece.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.180
He has appeared in a number of
feature-length documentaries,

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.690
including the recently released

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.430
Amend: The Fight for America, 2021,

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:46.430
the Oscar nominated 13th, 2016,
and Slavery by Another Name.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.430
Khalil is an an award-winning
teacher at Harvard

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.890
and has received numerous
honors for his commitment

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:55.160
to public engagement,
such as Ebony Power 100

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.580
and the Distinguished Service Medal

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.100
from Columbia University's
Teacher's College.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.820
He serves on several boards,

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.190
including the Vera Institute of Justice,

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.680
Cure Violence Global, and
the Museum of Modern Art.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:11.640
And just before I hand things
over to Professor Muhammad,

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.330
he's going to speak for 45 to 50 minutes.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.580
Then, Professor Snyder and
I, the co-teachers, conveners

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.470
of the seminar, will talk with
him for five minutes or so

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:25.270
while you put questions
and the question box.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:28.310
And then, we will moderate a 45 minute

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.750
question session with your
questions to Professor Muhammad.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:34.623
Okay, thank you.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:38.940
- Thank you very much, Jason.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:43.450
Thank you, Jason,
Professor Snyder, to Anna,

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.500
to all the people who
support this lecture series.

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:52.500
It's indeed an honor to visit
this seminar series, as well

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:57.180
as to be part of the Yale
virtual community this evening.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:00.020
I have a lot of friends there, and indeed,

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:04.280
Jason mentioning the work of
the National Academy's report

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:05.710
on reducing racial inequalities

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.380
in the criminal justice system.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.630
I'm also joined by Tracy Mirrors

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:13.270
and Elizabeth Hinton, two
friends and colleagues

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:14.850
who are working very hard on this report.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:19.840
So, in any case, tonight's lecture is both

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.610
tied to the readings that
the students have read,

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:28.000
as well as some continuing
thoughts of my own

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.370
and borrowing from the work
of some of my colleagues

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:33.460
who continue to broaden our understanding

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.850
of the punitive roots of US society.

00:04:36.850 --> 00:04:38.840
And with that, I thought
it opened with a little bit

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.420
of a context setting,
drawing on newer scholarship

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:46.970
by my colleagues, Kelly Lytle
Hernandez and Dan Berger

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:50.620
about settler colonialism, and
then launch into a bit more

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:55.480
directed focus on the
topic of tonight's lecture.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.850
So with that, I'm going to
do just a tiny bit of reading

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:03.850
and then walk through the
material for this class.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:08.860
So, in many ways, lots of
people have been focused

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.880
on the question of why the
United States, at this moment

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.850
in history, is the most
punitive nation in the world.

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:20.300
And of course, it's almost
rote to say that by population,

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.340
by per capita rates and by expenditures,

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:24.940
the United States exceeds
all other nations in how many

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:28.320
of its citizens, asylum seekers
and undocumented immigrants,

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.670
are under some form of
criminal legal supervision.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.980
And over the past two decades,
there has been an explosion

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.490
of reports by government
agencies, nonprofits,

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:40.640
institutional advocacy,
organizations, so on and so forth,

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.443
exploring the dimensions
of what some would argue,

00:05:51.144 --> 00:05:52.180
and while these empirical
and comparative data

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:53.940
on the size and scope of this system

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:55.970
and its many quote-unquote
clients will continue

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:57.960
to lie at the heart of
many of these reports,

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.350
only within the past
decade, I'd say, have people

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:04.160
really begun to take history seriously.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:08.040
And so, answering the question
which I think animates

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.040
much of the seminar,
why the United States,

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.850
by comparison to, say, the Soviet Union,

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:17.830
is so punitive may be the most
relevant question to answer,

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:21.740
and not simply to document
its size, scale and scope.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.350
And the challenge of
answering this question,

00:06:23.350 --> 00:06:25.880
making it perhaps the most
difficult one to settle,

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.660
is not the lack of various
historical drivers or causes,

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.260
or even the range of possible
philosophical explanations

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:34.620
for how Americans have
imagined crime, sin,

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:36.610
human nature, and the
utility of punishment

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:38.700
going back to the Enlightenment period.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.950
The problem is of course
that these historical

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.890
and philosophical
explanations have often been

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:45.880
least credible or
authoritative in explaining

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.610
mass incarceration among policymakers.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:51.220
And this seems to me
to be a larger problem

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.840
about empiricism, about the relationship

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.370
of criminal justice
practitioners and their role

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:00.000
in providing technical assistance,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.320
a kind of coziness that has
emerged over the past 50 years

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:07.040
with regard to the study of crime

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:10.150
and criminalization and
the actual practitioners

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:15.150
and law enforcement courts,
as well as in corrections.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.840
And therefore, the disconnect
between the primary

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.720
knowledge producers of criminal justice

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.720
and just about everyone
else has grown wider.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:28.240
And so, one of the legacies
of this disconnection

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:33.050
is the dismissal of history
as an important tool

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:35.760
to understand how we got here.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:40.760
That, as I've already suggested,
is beginning to change.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:45.240
So, let me set the scene,
a kind of long history

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:49.470
that I think helps to put
my more immediate concerns

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.790
for this evening's talk in context.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:54.650
From the beginning, the
United States has been

00:07:54.650 --> 00:07:58.820
what the historian Dan Berger
calls a captive nation.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:02.620
In the summer of 2019,
of course, many people

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.770
within the United States
and in some places

00:08:05.623 --> 00:08:08.733
around the world, commemorate
the 400th anniversary

00:08:08.733 --> 00:08:12.410
of at least the landing of 20
or so Africans in Virginia,

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:16.400
and that this moment
represented a kind of attempt

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:19.620
to reflect on the fact
that in American history,

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.110
there is no moment when
European-descended people

00:08:24.110 --> 00:08:27.160
did not use racialized
forms of punishment,

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.430
war or containment
against indigenous tribes,

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:33.160
immigrants or enslaved
people of African descent.

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:35.920
Settler colonists first use
the logic of elimination,

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:38.040
then turn to ideas of exploitation

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:41.270
to make way for their permanent residency.

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.750
Two and a half centuries
before the nation was founded,

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.210
and for nearly a century
after, the core institutions

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:48.330
of American democracy
and economy were built

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.250
on the land of the indigenous
and the backs of the enslaved.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:53.397
As Dan Berger writes, quote, "Race,

00:08:53.397 --> 00:08:57.197
"especially anti-black racism,
has been the primary modality

00:08:57.197 --> 00:09:00.737
"through which this pairing of
colonization and confinement

00:09:00.737 --> 00:09:03.097
"has transpired in the United States.

00:09:03.097 --> 00:09:05.547
"Forcible consignment haunted black life

00:09:05.547 --> 00:09:08.647
"from capture in Africa
through the Middle Passage

00:09:08.647 --> 00:09:10.290
"and sale in the Americas."

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.017
And he continues, "Chattel slavery

00:09:12.017 --> 00:09:15.327
"initiated a racial regime
rooted and confinement.

00:09:15.327 --> 00:09:16.907
"Plantation slavery was as much

00:09:16.907 --> 00:09:20.123
"a carceral force as
the early penitentiary.

00:09:21.027 --> 00:09:23.547
"The historical institutions
of native reservations

00:09:23.547 --> 00:09:26.207
"and African-American slavery
were the most durable legacies

00:09:26.207 --> 00:09:29.417
"of a number of ideas and
ideologies that helped forge

00:09:29.417 --> 00:09:32.517
"the punitive foundations
of American society.

00:09:32.517 --> 00:09:34.597
"The frontier myth of
a virgin land waiting

00:09:34.597 --> 00:09:36.817
"to be tamed and
cultivated by a master race

00:09:36.817 --> 00:09:39.053
"animated much of the
colonial justification

00:09:39.053 --> 00:09:41.387
'for native displacement and genocide.

00:09:41.387 --> 00:09:44.057
"As generation after
generation of white colonists

00:09:44.057 --> 00:09:46.967
"and later citizens moved
West, the choice to define

00:09:46.967 --> 00:09:49.657
"native populations
and Mexicans as, quote,

00:09:49.657 --> 00:09:53.477
"'savages or criminals by
law, custom, or practice,'

00:09:53.477 --> 00:09:56.320
"rationalized the eventual creation

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:57.870
"of the nation from sea to sea.

00:09:59.108 --> 00:10:02.077
"That Europeans did not
encounter the legal restrictions

00:10:02.077 --> 00:10:05.107
"and physical constraints
of the old world and turned

00:10:05.107 --> 00:10:07.687
"to religion to justify
conquest gave them a sense

00:10:07.687 --> 00:10:10.867
"of legitimacy to what they
called Manifest Destiny.

00:10:10.867 --> 00:10:13.357
"Philosophers and political
theorists from Adam Smith

00:10:13.357 --> 00:10:15.787
"to John Locke and Thomas Hobbes

00:10:15.787 --> 00:10:19.787
"helped justify conquest as
the march of civilization.

00:10:19.787 --> 00:10:22.907
"By the 19th Century, a system
of federalism had evolved,

00:10:22.907 --> 00:10:25.757
"which maximized various
states monopolies on violence

00:10:25.757 --> 00:10:29.077
"to ensure conquered
indigenous and Mexican land

00:10:29.077 --> 00:10:32.337
"would be converted to
private property by whites

00:10:32.337 --> 00:10:36.000
"and capitalized by
chattelized black people."

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.812
And so, what is at stake in
this sketch of the early history

00:10:40.812 --> 00:10:43.100
of the United States is precisely,

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:45.313
and let me pause here and say this slowly,

00:10:51.623 --> 00:10:52.690
of the law itself.

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:55.360
At all times, a white outlaw culture

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.090
that rewarded brute force and strength

00:10:57.090 --> 00:10:59.950
of arms against racialized
others unsettles basic

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:01.980
assumptions about how we are to understand

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.800
criminalization and
punitiveness over time,

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.530
that is who is counted as
criminal and to what extent

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:10.990
the state has used violence or punishment.

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:12.930
In Kelly Lytle Hernandez's recent study,

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:14.770
City of Inmates: Conquests, Rebellion,

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:17.510
and the Rise of Human
Caging in Los Angeles,

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:20.210
for which, among many
other accomplishments,

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:22.390
she won a MacArthur Genius Award,

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:24.250
she found a remarkably stable pattern

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:26.220
of criminalization and
incarceration going back

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:29.310
to a single carceral
site in the Tanga Basin,

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:31.900
once part of the Mexico named
for the native community

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:35.830
that originally occupied the
land for seven millennia.

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:38.660
What is today modern Los
Angeles, and here, I'll begin

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:41.753
to shift to this portion of the lecture,

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:45.900
if someone could just let me know

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:48.890
that you see only the slide
and not a bunch of noise

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:51.360
in the background, that would be helpful.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:53.410
But if you do, here, why don't I do this?

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:56.930
Sorry for the pause, I know this is always

00:11:56.930 --> 00:12:00.230
a little awkward when you're
working with one screen.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:02.493
So, you should see mostly the slide.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:07.060
What you see there is a depiction

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:10.330
of a 19th Century Los Angeles jail.

00:12:10.330 --> 00:12:14.040
And part of what you want
to draw your attention to

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:17.300
is the seamlessness with which the space

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:21.310
of incarceration fits
within the larger commerce,

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:24.113
an economy of this growing city.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:29.970
What she writes is that what
is today modern Los Angeles,

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.770
crime, punishment, emerged as the platform

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:35.290
for the racialized
inequities established during

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:39.100
the colonial era to flourish
in the Republic of Mexico.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:41.930
By the 1820s, the new
nation's jails, prisons

00:12:41.930 --> 00:12:44.150
and convict labor crews were overcrowded

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:47.410
with the historically marginalized,
the old colonial order,

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:49.630
largely a population of Natives, Africans,

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:52.520
mulattoes and mestizos, she writes.

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:54.660
As displaced landless
people, they were rested

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:58.260
on public order charges
ranging from vagrancy,

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:00.480
disorderly conduct and drunkenness.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:02.280
She notes that in order
for manifest destiny

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:04.160
to have become more than a proclamation

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:06.600
or a simple fact of conquest by treaty,

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.310
then the law needed an infrastructure.

00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:10.430
And let me note here that the arc of time

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:14.580
between charges of disorderly
conduct or drunkenness,

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:17.910
or what would later be
called status offenses

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:20.873
will have a powerful resonance
later in this lecture.

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:24.260
She notes that the law
needed an infrastructure.

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:25.950
The local jail therefore represented

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:27.870
the foundational structure, she writes,

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:30.660
of US conquest in Los Angeles.

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:35.370
She continues, it was how the
rule of law was established.

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:38.360
By 1850, the city passed
an ordinance that deputized

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:42.710
all whites on complaint
of any reasonable citizen,

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:45.360
and of course, the very
definition of citizen excluded

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:48.560
the very groups who were being
incarcerated, and established

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:51.320
racialized municipal chain
gangs and convict leasing

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:54.483
nearly a generation
before the end of slavery.

00:13:56.140 --> 00:14:00.510
And so, let's talk about that
moment at the end of slavery,

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:04.090
and let's clarify a few terms
that I think are helpful,

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:05.850
if they've not already been made clear

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:07.893
in the context of previous discussions,

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:10.980
We talk a lot about mass incarceration.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:13.640
I use the term racial criminalization,

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.520
and I want to introduce a concept

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:18.510
of mass criminalization
that may be less familiar.

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:20.110
And in this way, racial criminalization

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:23.490
is simply defining or stigmatizing
a racial group of people

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:26.380
as criminal, regardless
of guilt or innocence.

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:29.010
Of course, there's a
little bit of a double use

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:31.190
of the term racial here,
but the point being

00:14:31.190 --> 00:14:34.520
that a racialized group
of people can then,

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:37.970
with the power of prescription,
be defined as criminal,

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:40.510
and it doesn't matter,
individual behavior,

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:42.740
it is a form of collective guilt.

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:44.500
Mass criminalization, and that is

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:46.610
using law and policy to limit the freedom

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:48.500
of a group of people
regardless of innocence.

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:53.500
And so here, we move into what
we will learn more quickly

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:57.670
is one thing to assign a
stereotype to a group of people.

00:14:57.670 --> 00:14:59.090
It is another thing to use the force

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:01.270
of the law to act on that stereotype.

00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:03.110
And then finally, mass incarceration.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:04.570
And this is an important distinction,

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:06.510
because mass criminalization does not

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:08.630
by definition lead to mass incarceration.

00:15:08.630 --> 00:15:10.340
Indeed, what we'll learn in just a moment,

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:13.010
or what you likely already
know, is that the era

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:15.650
of convict leasing in
the South and the era

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:19.890
of all-white juries after the
passage of the 14th Amendment

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:22.670
is a period of mass criminalization.

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:27.010
The law is intended as
a form of racial control

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:30.860
or social coercion within a
political economy of punishment.

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:33.670
The point is that people will accept

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:36.120
their second-class citizenship,
or they will accept

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:39.620
the coercive regimes of
sharecropping or tenant farming,

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:43.450
or other forms of extractive
work, having little to do

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.673
with putting as many people behind bars,

00:15:50.150 --> 00:15:51.860
where obviously, we're talking about

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:54.320
in the mid-to-late 19th century, in fact.

00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:57.580
The infrastructure of the jail
itself was less substantial

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:00.523
than was the prison farm
or the private lease.

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:03.400
But most people simply
didn't serve time at all.

00:16:03.400 --> 00:16:07.170
They were scared into accepting
their second-class lives

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:12.170
working as free labor on a
cotton farm or cotton plantation.

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:15.100
And so, of course, the 13th
Amendment, which has come

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:18.360
into lots of scrutiny,
both for activists calling

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:22.590
for new amendments to strike
the clause of punishment

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:25.840
for crime, but also
some scholarly scrutiny

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:30.195
for a question of whether Congress

00:16:30.195 --> 00:16:33.440
or the radical Republicans intended

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:36.980
to make the 13th Amendment
a kind of hollow gesture.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:39.400
Nowhere in my work have I ascribed

00:16:39.400 --> 00:16:41.743
that intentionality to the 13th Amendment.

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:45.260
Although I don't write about
it, it's certainly acknowledged

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:49.260
that this kind of legal
language or legal loophole

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:52.860
for forums of social and
civil death had been key

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:55.660
to the notion of hard work
as a form of punishment

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:59.270
and labor, even when prisons
were racially disparate

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:02.980
in the opposite direction
in the early 19th Century,

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:05.790
where they were simply all white prisons.

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:08.830
But that doesn't change the
fact that in the context

00:17:08.830 --> 00:17:11.450
of the South and the postbellum period,

00:17:11.450 --> 00:17:14.160
southerners made quite creative use

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:17.000
of this loophole for their own purposes

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:22.000
and enshrined in a set of
laws, known as black codes,

00:17:22.460 --> 00:17:24.590
that African-Americans would be subjected

00:17:24.590 --> 00:17:29.130
to an entire body of legal
jurisprudence that would be,

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:32.800
at its core, anti-black
and intended to be so.

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:36.940
The context of this is also a
kind of political revolution

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:40.230
that brought with it new
justification for ideas.

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:43.170
And these ideas are very
important because the interplay

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:48.060
of the use of various forms of coercive

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:51.550
criminal justice practices,
including the leasing of people

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:55.120
to private industry or the
threat of punishment as such,

00:17:55.120 --> 00:18:00.120
also was wed to a narrative of blackness

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:02.890
that quickly attached to new forms

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:05.960
of data, essentially, to make the case.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:10.210
But I wanted to highlight
here that the ascription

00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:15.210
of criminality as a new form
of justifying oppression

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:18.510
is also the ongoing control over power,

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:22.190
the control over the power
of political representation

00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:25.060
and participation, which,
of course, would come

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:27.270
with citizenship and
eventually be legislated

00:18:27.270 --> 00:18:29.810
by virtue of needing to
pass another amendment.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:31.080
That is the 15th Amendment.

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:34.360
And here, we just hear the
voice of one of the key writers

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:37.930
of this period, Hinton Rowan
Helper, essentially warning

00:18:37.930 --> 00:18:40.820
the nation of what happens
if black people get to vote

00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:42.560
and that their criminality is the basis

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:45.230
for that distract disfranchisement
and that distrust.

00:18:45.230 --> 00:18:47.450
Negroes with their crime-stained blackness

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:49.860
could not rise to a plain
any higher than that

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.150
of base and beast-like savagery.

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:54.780
Helper presented his
collection of expert opinions

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:58.240
as an archeologist to use
his fossils to reconstruct

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:00.240
some prehistoric creature
for the world to behold,

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:02.360
with gratitude that had
no longer walks the earth.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:04.860
In Helpers case, the
caption for posterity read,

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:06.460
terrible things await a nation

00:19:06.460 --> 00:19:08.580
bent on handing ballots to beasts.

00:19:08.580 --> 00:19:10.980
Seeing then end of the negro
does indeed belong to a lower

00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:13.460
and inferior order of beings,
why in the name of heaven,

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:14.810
why, he pleaded with his readers,

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:17.450
should we forever degrade
and disgrace both ourselves

00:19:17.450 --> 00:19:20.080
and our posterity by
entering of our own volition

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:21.920
into more intimate relations with him?

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:23.800
May God and his restraining mercy forbid

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:26.420
that we should ever do this
most foul and wicked thing.

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:28.040
Now, I know you've read chapter two.

00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:30.150
This comes from chapter one.

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.360
But it helps to establish the baseline

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.290
for the ways in which
the notion of savagery

00:19:36.290 --> 00:19:39.230
also led to extra-legal violence.

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:42.380
And so, if in the context
of the postbellum period,

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:45.250
if in the context for
an explosion of the use

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:46.840
of the criminal justice machinery,

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:49.320
or as Kelly Lytle Hernandez
has already suggested to us,

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:53.130
the infrastructure of the
jail as a coercive instrument,

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:55.290
so, too, were forms of racial terror.

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:59.260
And here is just one slice of
looking at documented cases

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:01.340
of lynchings across multiple states.

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:03.410
This number continues
to change as researchers

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:05.460
continue to find a new examples.

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.640
The range, of course, many people now know

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:12.420
was very arbitrary acts of transgressing

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:15.630
the social norms of white
supremacy in the South.

00:20:15.630 --> 00:20:19.210
Because as much as the exploitative regime

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:22.670
of sharecropping was
intended to use the threat

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:26.300
of punishment to coerce
people, it also was wrapped

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:29.120
in the notion that people's own dignity

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:32.370
was a threat to those forms of oppression.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:36.710
The simple fact of communicating
across the color line,

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:40.600
walking with one's back and
dignity wearing a uniform

00:20:40.600 --> 00:20:44.090
of World War I, extended the range

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:47.227
of the kinds of things policed initially.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:56.980
And so, we began to accumulate
not just the evidence

00:20:57.070 --> 00:21:01.090
of racial terror, which
often was read as evidence

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:04.710
of a black transgression
or of a crime committed

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:08.890
by a black person, and in
the most extreme examples

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:13.890
used to justify lynching,
was the accusation of rape,

00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:16.530
black male rape of white women.

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:19.540
And so, we began to produce
statistical evidence

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:23.310
out of what today we'd call
criminal justice contact,

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:27.470
police contact, convictions,
arrests, these sorts of things.

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:29.540
And so now, with the
distance of hindsight,

00:21:29.540 --> 00:21:32.790
no one within the sound of
my voice would likely say,

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:37.790
well, of course, that was
all objective state activity,

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:39.490
and therefore the numbers were

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:41.730
a helpful guide to
understanding the levels

00:21:41.730 --> 00:21:44.433
of antisocial behavior
within the black community.

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:50.270
But I'd caution you to so easily judge

00:21:50.270 --> 00:21:52.550
those who articulated this
point of view in the past,

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:55.930
because it's precisely the
durability of this notion

00:21:55.930 --> 00:22:00.020
that crime statistics have a
kind of truth value to them

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:03.070
that has helped not
only to justify the way

00:22:03.070 --> 00:22:06.010
that the South first, and then really,

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:08.220
at a national scale organized
around these issues.

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:10.010
And even saying the South
first is complicated,

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:12.570
because the treatment of
antebellum African-Americans

00:22:12.570 --> 00:22:15.670
as a criminal class even
proceeded the postbellum period.

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:19.600
But at scale, and within the
context of national debates

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:24.200
about the relative fitness of
black people for citizenship,

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:27.690
we first get to mass
emancipation in the period

00:22:27.690 --> 00:22:30.930
after the Civil War, and then new kinds

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:33.700
of tools that take place, new kinds

00:22:33.700 --> 00:22:36.810
of demographic questions and concerns.

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:38.730
This is the heyday of eugenics,

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:42.410
population studies are
happening in the Western world

00:22:42.410 --> 00:22:44.470
in part to figure out how to manage

00:22:44.470 --> 00:22:48.140
and contain the movement
and the aspirations

00:22:48.140 --> 00:22:51.580
of the global South, as we
would recognize it today.

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:55.910
And so, Nathaniel Shaler
happens to be a popularizer

00:22:55.910 --> 00:22:58.530
of history, a person who's
writing for the Atlantic Monthly,

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:02.260
the same magazine that
exists today with the same

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:04.580
kind of approach and
tone, which is to bridge

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:08.020
the gap between social science and policy,

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:10.030
and important questions
that people are trying

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:13.570
to answer with empirical
data for popular audiences.

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:16.240
And so, as a Harvard
paleontologist, he was a naturalist.

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:19.270
He had no particular skillset
for this kind of work,

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:21.810
but took it on as a translator.

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:24.290
And in his first article on this topic,

00:23:24.290 --> 00:23:27.860
he essentially turned to
statistics as a way to resolve

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:31.190
a kind of problem, and that
problem was articulated

00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:36.190
by Helper, which was to
say that many people assume

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:41.300
that southerners had a kind
of a bias towards ascribing

00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:46.050
criminality and other racist
stereotypes about black people,

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:48.210
that they were primitive,
and that in the position

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:50.870
of being free, they were retrograding

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:53.173
to their quote-unquote
ancestral condition.

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:57.240
I don't want to get too much
into the present right now,

00:23:57.240 --> 00:24:01.340
but to simply to say that
northerners had the reputation

00:24:01.340 --> 00:24:04.080
of being sympathetic to black people.

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:08.150
And in the postbellum period,
these sectional divisions

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:11.670
about whose knowledge
was most authoritative

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:14.270
on this question of the appropriate place

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:16.010
that black people would fit in the social

00:24:16.010 --> 00:24:18.690
and political hierarchy of the nation

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:22.290
came to be resolved by new forms of data.

00:24:22.290 --> 00:24:25.350
And I'm dwelling on this point
for just a moment longer,

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:29.500
because it's precisely the
eugenics period where the notion

00:24:29.500 --> 00:24:32.830
of objective crime data
or IQ test or birth rates

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:35.870
could all be forms of knowledge production

00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:39.270
that would help policy
makers and elected officials

00:24:39.270 --> 00:24:42.490
and other decision-makers
to make sure that they

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:46.020
organized society the most
productive and efficient way.

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:48.290
And so, out of the statistical data

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:50.410
that Nathaniel Shaler was calling for

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:55.410
came the first turn towards
the US census in 1990.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:59.060
This census is an important milestone,

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:01.987
because it represented a
perfect generational cohort

00:25:01.987 --> 00:25:06.070
for looking at the health and welfare

00:25:06.070 --> 00:25:09.650
of African-Americans who had
been born outside of slavery.

00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:13.300
So, if you're born in 1866, for example,

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:16.760
or in the winter of 1865, you've never,

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:19.260
quote-unquote, been touched by slavery,

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:23.240
and you could essentially
compare the health,

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:27.000
mortality, morbidity,
crime rates of people

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:29.740
who had never been as late
to those who had had been.

00:25:29.740 --> 00:25:32.190
This kind of comparison
didn't actually happen

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:35.210
in the way that I've
described, but general claims

00:25:35.210 --> 00:25:40.210
did occur, meaning that
people took to the 1890 Census

00:25:40.370 --> 00:25:44.040
as the first authoritative
census after the Civil War.

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:47.613
One, sampling techniques
were problematic in 18 seven.

00:25:52.240 --> 00:25:54.060
There was now the famous writer,

00:25:54.060 --> 00:25:56.640
Thomas Dixon Page, who was
the novelist who's work,

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:59.160
The Klansman became the
basis of Birth of a Nation,

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:01.220
he and others called the New Issue.

00:26:01.220 --> 00:26:04.300
And the New Issue was a younger
generation of black people.

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:07.080
Remember, someone like
W.E.B Du Bois is born

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:10.130
in Great Barrington,
Massachusetts in 1868.

00:26:10.130 --> 00:26:11.880
He represents the New Issue.

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:15.800
He represents a person
who sees the possibilities

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:19.780
of his full humanity and being unchecked

00:26:19.780 --> 00:26:23.750
and undiminished by
claims of white supremacy.

00:26:23.750 --> 00:26:26.850
And essentially, this
animates his entire life

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:29.210
in corpus of academic work.

00:26:29.210 --> 00:26:32.770
The other new issue, of course,
is all those entrepreneurial

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:35.560
and aspiring black people in the South

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:39.580
who are moving with freedom
and mobility in the South

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:42.200
and challenging, again, the
norms of white supremacy,

00:26:42.200 --> 00:26:46.330
of which we saw the both
being defined as criminal,

00:26:46.330 --> 00:26:49.150
and sometimes literally
being lynched by a lynch mob.

00:26:49.150 --> 00:26:54.150
And so, the 1890 census
produces this new source

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:59.620
of administrative data that
points to a snapshot in time

00:27:00.580 --> 00:27:04.050
that the population of
African-Americans is now 12%,

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:06.260
but they represent 30% of those

00:27:06.260 --> 00:27:08.920
that could be counted as prisoners.

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:12.370
The raw numbers are roughly
82,000 total prisoners,

00:27:12.370 --> 00:27:14.540
of which black people are about 22,000.

00:27:14.540 --> 00:27:18.280
Within the 22,000, about
90% of them are men.

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:22.020
Now, because this is a
critique of these data,

00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:23.570
the point is not to dwell on those

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:26.410
particular statistics,
to check their veracity.

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:29.430
It didn't really matter,
because people who turned

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:32.860
to those statistics, for
the first time who made use

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:36.180
of those statistics, who
saw themselves as innovative

00:27:36.180 --> 00:27:40.790
in an effort to pick up where
Nathaniel Shaler had left off

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:43.180
by calling for statistics
as a way of measuring

00:27:43.180 --> 00:27:45.720
the true racial capacity of black people,

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:49.070
one of those figures was a
man named Frederick Hoffman,

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:51.030
and in his work, Race
Trace and Tendencies,

00:27:51.030 --> 00:27:53.870
which is really a work
about health differences

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:57.360
and health outcomes, because
in his day job as an actuary,

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:00.080
as the leading actuary
for Prudential Insurance,

00:28:00.080 --> 00:28:04.740
his job was to make predictions
about life and death,

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:08.390
so that insurance policies
could be priced accordingly.

00:28:08.390 --> 00:28:13.390
He also had a particular
concern about making sure

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:17.840
that working class white
people, including immigrants

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:20.580
like himself, he was a German immigrant,

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:23.150
would get a fair deal
because he recognized

00:28:23.150 --> 00:28:27.280
that both nativism and segregation

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:30.930
of limited housing
opportunities, limited mobility,

00:28:30.930 --> 00:28:35.890
various forms of
state-sponsored discrimination

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:38.860
directed towards immigrant populations,

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:40.513
was causing a problem for them.

00:28:41.410 --> 00:28:43.670
And Hoffman's innovation in this moment

00:28:43.670 --> 00:28:48.040
is to single out African-Americans,

00:28:48.040 --> 00:28:51.200
to lift them out of what might have been.

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:53.523
And I'm not arguing a
counterfactual, I'm simply arguing

00:28:53.523 --> 00:28:57.080
the debate that emerges
around Hoffman's work.

00:28:57.080 --> 00:29:01.450
What might have been a robust sociological

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:04.580
or ecological argument about the nature

00:29:04.580 --> 00:29:07.963
of premature death in the modern city,

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:12.350
what essentially he wanted to say was that

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:13.727
many immigrants are suffering from

00:29:13.727 --> 00:29:18.720
exogenous social forces,
but black people are not.

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:22.180
And to make his point,
he made what we call

00:29:22.180 --> 00:29:24.850
a white supremacist argument today.

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:27.330
He essentially argued
that black people by dent

00:29:27.330 --> 00:29:31.860
of behavior and biology
faced only two options

00:29:31.860 --> 00:29:35.240
in the race of competition
with white people,

00:29:35.240 --> 00:29:39.150
that is the road to prison
or to an early grave.

00:29:39.150 --> 00:29:41.810
And he was using the
data I just showed you

00:29:41.810 --> 00:29:46.190
of the disproportionate
representation of black people

00:29:46.190 --> 00:29:48.420
in prison as evidence for it.

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:51.630
Now, of course, you know
that the political economy

00:29:51.630 --> 00:29:53.980
of punishment, which
we've already established,

00:29:53.980 --> 00:29:57.480
makes it nearly impossible
to know how many

00:29:57.480 --> 00:29:59.960
black people were truly
quote-unquote guilty

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:03.170
of a crime and deserving of punishment.

00:30:03.170 --> 00:30:07.763
What we do know is that
generalized notions of the

00:30:09.350 --> 00:30:12.360
structural forces contributing to crime

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:15.780
was an increasingly popular point of view,

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:18.800
pushing back against eugenics claims.

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:22.190
And so, here Hoffman
clearly positions himself

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:26.980
as someone interested
in progressive solutions

00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:29.770
to the problem of crime and other forms

00:30:29.770 --> 00:30:32.790
of antisocial or community-based harm.

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:34.990
He writes four years before Race Traits

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:36.700
and Tendencies of the American Negro,

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:39.100
the study of statistics of
suicide madness and crime

00:30:39.100 --> 00:30:41.110
is one of the utmost
importance to any society.

00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:43.030
When such an increase
has been proved to exist,

00:30:43.030 --> 00:30:45.640
it is the duty of society to
leave nothing undone until

00:30:45.640 --> 00:30:48.440
the evil has been checked or
been brought under control,

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:53.730
must come with the wealth of people.

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:55.580
We must be far from truth
lies, as long as we permit

00:30:55.580 --> 00:30:57.480
to exist or accept inevitable conditions,

00:30:57.480 --> 00:30:59.070
which year after you're
drive an increasing army

00:30:59.070 --> 00:31:01.330
of unfortunates to
madness, crime or suicide.

00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:03.800
It is the struggle of the
masses against the classes.

00:31:03.800 --> 00:31:08.590
I like to joke that if Bernie
Sanders needed a speech writer

00:31:08.590 --> 00:31:12.720
to illustrate the excesses of capitalism

00:31:12.720 --> 00:31:15.150
and the harm that it does to people,

00:31:15.150 --> 00:31:17.940
this metaphor of an increasing
army of unfortunates

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:21.920
would resonate very
powerfully with concern

00:31:21.920 --> 00:31:25.010
about the premature death
of white people suffering

00:31:25.010 --> 00:31:30.010
from both de-industrialization
and the opioid crisis.

00:31:31.230 --> 00:31:33.560
So, Hoffman was not alone.

00:31:33.560 --> 00:31:36.410
There were others, such as the socialist

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:38.780
and Progressive Labor Party
member, Harry Vrooman,

00:31:38.780 --> 00:31:42.700
writing in the same magazine,
also before Race Traits,

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:46.000
in an article called Crime
and the Enforcement of Law,

00:31:46.000 --> 00:31:48.670
that the whole problem of crime,
as to expressed in society

00:31:48.670 --> 00:31:50.170
is summed up in the problem of poverty.

00:31:50.170 --> 00:31:51.650
We have churches enough, schools enough,

00:31:51.650 --> 00:31:53.540
moral sentiment enough,
to regenerate the world

00:31:53.540 --> 00:31:55.810
in a decade where it not
for the awful pressure

00:31:55.810 --> 00:31:58.530
brought to bear on
nine-tenths of the human race,

00:31:58.530 --> 00:32:01.270
which all but forces them to be vicious.

00:32:01.270 --> 00:32:04.860
And so, I want to establish a clear line

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:08.530
that in Hoffman's own
formulation of the use

00:32:08.530 --> 00:32:12.680
of crime statistics, we have
two divergent explanations,

00:32:12.680 --> 00:32:15.480
that the data of the
over-representation of people

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:18.450
in criminality when
applied to white people,

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:20.230
and European immigrants in particular,

00:32:20.230 --> 00:32:24.510
represents the evidence of social forces,

00:32:24.510 --> 00:32:29.060
of the structural economy, of
the excesses of capitalism,

00:32:29.060 --> 00:32:31.820
which is why these people are
self-identified as socialists

00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:35.300
and progressives who share
this point of view in common.

00:32:35.300 --> 00:32:39.280
But Hoffman argued just the
opposite for African-Americans,

00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:42.200
because as a racist and racialist project,

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:46.100
Hoffman pursued the data
to make the argument,

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:48.610
or I should say, to make
the data fit the argument.

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:51.410
Here, he notes prison and
arrest statistics from Chicago,

00:32:51.410 --> 00:32:53.490
Philadelphia, Louisville, and
Charleston, and from states

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:55.230
including New Jersey and Pennsylvania,

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:57.670
confirm the census data
and show without exception

00:32:57.670 --> 00:33:00.090
that the criminality of the
Negro exceeds that of any other

00:33:00.090 --> 00:33:02.690
race of any numerical
importance in the country.

00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:04.350
So, let's just take that first part

00:33:04.350 --> 00:33:06.380
of the statement at face value.

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:11.380
It is true that the evidence
of the disproportionate

00:33:12.053 --> 00:33:15.290
participation in the
criminal justice system

00:33:15.290 --> 00:33:18.070
at the arrest level and
in the confinement level

00:33:18.070 --> 00:33:21.210
show the highest rate
of disproportionality.

00:33:21.210 --> 00:33:22.880
But if we were to take the same logic

00:33:22.880 --> 00:33:25.490
as has been applied to
various white communities,

00:33:25.490 --> 00:33:27.640
not by the eugenicist,
but by the progressives

00:33:27.640 --> 00:33:31.040
and socialists, we say those
are the people suffering most

00:33:31.040 --> 00:33:34.050
from the structural disadvantages
that happen in society.

00:33:34.050 --> 00:33:36.190
But instead, Hoffman
said just the opposite.

00:33:36.190 --> 00:33:38.120
When the Negro learns to
respect life, property,

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:40.170
and chastity, until he learns
to believe in the value

00:33:40.170 --> 00:33:42.850
of a personal morality
operating in his daily life,

00:33:42.850 --> 00:33:45.830
the criminal tendencies will increase.

00:33:45.830 --> 00:33:48.160
And so, this notion of black criminality

00:33:48.160 --> 00:33:51.290
or racial criminalization, then happening

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:56.290
in massive form, takes
on a life of its own.

00:33:56.300 --> 00:34:01.100
And to use a concept
developed by Barbara Fields

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:04.690
called racecraft, essentially
the notion that new

00:34:06.460 --> 00:34:10.330
racial logics or aetiologies
of race are grafted

00:34:10.330 --> 00:34:15.330
onto ongoing projects of
racial control and power.

00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:20.060
And so in here, the case is the
degree to which black people

00:34:20.060 --> 00:34:22.530
are pursuing educational opportunities

00:34:22.530 --> 00:34:25.373
becomes an argument against
their further education.

00:34:26.570 --> 00:34:29.220
There is essentially all
sorts of magical thinking

00:34:29.220 --> 00:34:31.310
happening here, which is
why I love this concept

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:34.030
of racecraft that Barbara
Fields comes up with.

00:34:34.030 --> 00:34:35.490
But here's an example.

00:34:35.490 --> 00:34:38.050
The Mississippi Governor
James K. Vardaman,

00:34:38.050 --> 00:34:41.630
who is one of 11 Confederate governors,

00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:46.050
the former Confederate
states, and minces no words

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:49.090
about the degeneracy and
inferiority of black people.

00:34:49.090 --> 00:34:51.480
This is not a nuanced circumstance,

00:34:51.480 --> 00:34:54.180
but what's fascinating
here is he does in fact

00:34:55.160 --> 00:35:00.160
feel the pressure to defend
what is happening in Mississippi

00:35:00.790 --> 00:35:03.550
on the basis of crime statistics.

00:35:03.550 --> 00:35:07.610
And so, as much as there
is a robust, muscular

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:09.610
white supremacy happening on the ground,

00:35:09.610 --> 00:35:13.720
there is still this
growing appetite and need

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:16.200
for a kind of national
agreement that southerners

00:35:16.200 --> 00:35:19.670
may not be so bad after
all, if they can all agree

00:35:19.670 --> 00:35:21.950
that the crime statistics tell the story.

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:23.620
And so here, Governor Vardaman says,

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:25.480
to school the negro is to
increase his criminality.

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:27.420
Official statistics do
not lie, and they tell us

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:29.170
that the negroes who can read and write

00:35:29.170 --> 00:35:30.490
are more criminal than the illiterate.

00:35:30.490 --> 00:35:32.460
In New England, where
they are best educated,

00:35:32.460 --> 00:35:34.960
they are four-and-a-half times as criminal

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:36.830
as in the black belt, where
they are most ignorant.

00:35:36.830 --> 00:35:39.460
The more money for negro
education, the more negro crime.

00:35:39.460 --> 00:35:40.920
This is the unmistakable showing

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:42.150
of the United States census.

00:35:42.150 --> 00:35:44.040
So, I want to disrupt the easy way

00:35:44.040 --> 00:35:48.443
with which we might throw the
governor into a bucket of,

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:55.360
because even the governor wanted respect,

00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:59.240
he wanted this form of
propaganda to be heard as a way

00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:02.180
of justifying policies that were meant

00:36:02.180 --> 00:36:07.100
to be modern and future-oriented

00:36:07.100 --> 00:36:11.150
and not like his pro-slavery ancestors,

00:36:11.150 --> 00:36:13.930
in the same way that we learned a lot

00:36:13.930 --> 00:36:16.130
in international
comparisons about the ways

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:19.480
in which 20th century forms of segregation

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:22.420
and colonization are drawing on new ideas,

00:36:22.420 --> 00:36:26.923
or at least ideas that
are newly ascendant.

00:36:28.120 --> 00:36:30.900
And so, the black
sociologist Kelly Miller,

00:36:30.900 --> 00:36:33.770
who's at Howard University at the time,

00:36:33.770 --> 00:36:36.450
takes this argument on
directly, as does Du Bois

00:36:36.450 --> 00:36:38.820
in the Study of the Negro Problem,

00:36:38.820 --> 00:36:42.170
where he points out
Hoffman's biases by showing

00:36:42.170 --> 00:36:44.030
that there is an appetite
within the nation

00:36:44.030 --> 00:36:48.410
to accept without criticism
these early pronunciations

00:36:48.410 --> 00:36:51.360
of black people as forever
doomed to be criminal.

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:54.490
I'll talk a little bit more
about that in another example.

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:55.860
But here, Kelly Miller takes on

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:57.810
this exact argument at face value.

00:36:57.810 --> 00:36:59.610
He says, okay, here's the argument.

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:01.660
Where legal processes are
acknowledged to be fair,

00:37:01.660 --> 00:37:04.230
that is in the North, in
New England, for example,

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:06.987
where the negro has the fullest
educational opportunity,

00:37:06.987 --> 00:37:09.430
which was more like fuller
rather than fullest,

00:37:09.430 --> 00:37:12.200
he shows a criminal rate
three-to-four times as great

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:14.810
as his ignorant and oppressed
brother in the South.

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:16.440
And the conclusion is hastily reached

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:18.240
that education makes the negro criminal,

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:19.490
which of course is the justification

00:37:19.490 --> 00:37:22.150
for rolling back educational opportunity.

00:37:22.150 --> 00:37:25.370
So, Kelly Miller then goes
on to say, well, okay,

00:37:25.370 --> 00:37:27.760
if black men are five times
more likely in the state

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:30.170
of Massachusetts to serve
time than in Mississippi,

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:34.160
maybe Hoffman and others
are onto something.

00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:37.270
But what if white men are
10 times overrepresented?

00:37:37.270 --> 00:37:38.920
Which it turns out to be the case.

00:37:38.920 --> 00:37:40.510
And so, he asks this question,

00:37:40.510 --> 00:37:43.390
did education make northern
whites criminal too?

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:45.050
Or shall we foster the bliss of ignorance

00:37:45.050 --> 00:37:47.880
only when it is found under a black skin?

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:48.940
As you know from your reading,

00:37:48.940 --> 00:37:52.160
Du Bois takes on similar
arguments by pointing out

00:37:52.160 --> 00:37:56.030
in various European cities,
across from Munich to Breslau,

00:37:56.030 --> 00:37:59.310
to other places, rates of
quote-unquote illegitimacy

00:37:59.310 --> 00:38:03.820
and prostitution are higher
than is true for black women.

00:38:03.820 --> 00:38:07.400
You also know that as much
as Hoffman places his target

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:12.400
on the backs of black men, he
also uses illegitimacy rates

00:38:12.840 --> 00:38:17.550
and sexual transmission of
venereal diseases as evidence,

00:38:17.550 --> 00:38:20.660
essentially, the statistical equivalent

00:38:20.660 --> 00:38:24.510
for black women as sexually degenerate.

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:27.340
And so, these swirl of
arguments, of crime rates and

00:38:27.340 --> 00:38:31.680
illegitimacy rates contribute
to what is happening

00:38:31.680 --> 00:38:34.000
on the ground in cities
across the country.

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:36.760
In Atlanta, race riot emerges in 1906.

00:38:36.760 --> 00:38:40.440
A French journal captures the
scene in this illustration.

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:45.020
And what comes out of
that is a powerful example

00:38:45.020 --> 00:38:47.880
born out of the work of Ida B. Wells.

00:38:47.880 --> 00:38:50.660
From your reading, you know Ida B. Wells

00:38:50.660 --> 00:38:54.260
witnesses the lynching of three

00:38:54.260 --> 00:38:55.880
of her friends who run a grocery store.

00:38:55.880 --> 00:38:59.590
Out of that, she commits
herself to defending their honor

00:38:59.590 --> 00:39:01.970
and to challenging the racial terror

00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:05.520
of her hometown, Memphis, Tennessee.

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:09.670
Because she used her printing
press to tell their stories,

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:12.220
her printing press is
ultimately burned down

00:39:12.220 --> 00:39:14.680
and she leaves town one
step ahead of a lynch mob.

00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:17.700
She lands first in
Philadelphia, on to Chicago,

00:39:17.700 --> 00:39:19.910
where she will dedicate
the rest of her life

00:39:19.910 --> 00:39:22.860
not only exposing the
lies of white supremacy,

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:25.430
but building an infrastructure
of social service

00:39:25.430 --> 00:39:27.580
and political activism for black women

00:39:27.580 --> 00:39:29.410
in the club women's movement.

00:39:29.410 --> 00:39:31.190
She's an early founder of
the National Association of

00:39:31.190 --> 00:39:32.670
Colored Women, so on and so forth.

00:39:32.670 --> 00:39:34.860
So, she's got this incredible career,

00:39:34.860 --> 00:39:38.310
but she takes on these
arguments about lynching,

00:39:38.310 --> 00:39:42.530
because having borne
direct witness to its toll

00:39:42.530 --> 00:39:47.530
on black people, she is one
of the first African-Americans

00:39:48.860 --> 00:39:53.330
who are challenging the
use of crime statistics

00:39:53.330 --> 00:39:55.610
to make an argument
about black inferiority.

00:39:55.610 --> 00:39:59.010
In this example, she happens
to be at a fundraising dinner

00:39:59.010 --> 00:40:01.350
in Chicago where a speaker has just talked

00:40:01.350 --> 00:40:03.830
about the recent events in Atlanta,

00:40:03.830 --> 00:40:06.820
and a white woman named Mary Plummer,

00:40:06.820 --> 00:40:09.510
who's a suffragist leans
over to her and says,

00:40:09.510 --> 00:40:12.140
I don't know what we could say
about this terrible affair,

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:13.370
but there's one thing I can say,

00:40:13.370 --> 00:40:14.490
and that is to urge all of you

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:16.700
to drive the criminals out from among you.

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:19.070
Have you forgotten that 10%
of all the crimes committed

00:40:19.070 --> 00:40:21.660
in Chicago last year were by colored men,

00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:24.090
less than 3% of the population?

00:40:24.090 --> 00:40:27.230
It's a remarkable moment, because it shows

00:40:27.230 --> 00:40:30.450
that the disproportionate evidence

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:33.030
of criminal justice
contact for black people

00:40:33.030 --> 00:40:36.600
becomes a justification
for the instrumental use

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:38.403
of racial terror and violence.

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:43.220
Two things can be true at the same time,

00:40:43.220 --> 00:40:46.000
nevermind the fact that we can't trust

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:48.810
that there isn't bias in the

00:40:53.100 --> 00:40:55.900
logs and direct board
Americans in Chicago at this

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:58.440
time period, which we'll talk
about in just a few moments.

00:40:58.440 --> 00:41:00.710
And so within the body of
work, that Ida B. Wells

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:02.700
is involved in, her most
seminal work, Southern Horrors,

00:41:02.700 --> 00:41:07.550
which you've also read,
is a fascinating example

00:41:07.550 --> 00:41:10.460
that at no point in these
histories that we are telling

00:41:10.460 --> 00:41:15.460
did black people not speak
back to the ideologies

00:41:16.420 --> 00:41:20.180
and the behavior that contributed

00:41:20.180 --> 00:41:24.260
to premature death and
the justification of it.

00:41:24.260 --> 00:41:29.260
And so, in this study, she does
a sample of Chicago Tribune.

00:41:29.930 --> 00:41:31.830
She reads them, as you well know.

00:41:31.830 --> 00:41:34.800
I liken her approach here, a form

00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:37.750
of investigative journalism,
like the Washington Post

00:41:37.750 --> 00:41:40.300
or The Guardian keeping
track of police killings,

00:41:40.300 --> 00:41:43.082
because the nation is
not keeping track of it.

00:41:43.082 --> 00:41:46.040
And so, she wants to uncover the myth

00:41:46.040 --> 00:41:49.290
of black men as rapists as
justification for lynching,

00:41:49.290 --> 00:41:53.110
which you also know in your
reading of Frederick Hoffman,

00:41:53.110 --> 00:41:56.220
simply counted every lynching
as evidence of a black rape,

00:41:56.220 --> 00:41:59.440
which by his own standards,
add to our standards,

00:41:59.440 --> 00:42:02.310
simply wouldn't hold up to
peer review, so to speak.

00:42:02.310 --> 00:42:04.760
But nevertheless, Ida B. Wells says

00:42:04.760 --> 00:42:06.730
in the preamble of this work that out

00:42:06.730 --> 00:42:10.020
of their mouths shall
murderers be condemned,

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:12.770
and she goes on to debunk the rape myth.

00:42:12.770 --> 00:42:16.270
Now, of course, my colleague
Crystal Feimster at Yale

00:42:16.270 --> 00:42:19.240
has done wonderful work in
pointing out that this rape myth

00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:21.850
is as much a form of sexual control

00:42:21.850 --> 00:42:24.300
over the behavior of white women.

00:42:24.300 --> 00:42:27.790
And so, we have to think about
the gendered implications

00:42:27.790 --> 00:42:30.440
of white supremacy, both as
directed towards black women,

00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:33.680
but as well that this myth also functioned

00:42:33.680 --> 00:42:36.710
as a form of sexual and social control

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:39.120
over white women's sexuality, as well.

00:42:39.120 --> 00:42:42.000
She debunks the myth
by noting that only 31%

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:44.190
of even the allegation in those newspapers

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:46.870
was of rape, and she also goes on

00:42:46.870 --> 00:42:49.750
to debunk the myth of black
female licentiousness.

00:42:49.750 --> 00:42:52.310
In this way, she inverts
a racial narrative

00:42:52.310 --> 00:42:55.170
by reporting on white
male rape of black women,

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:57.270
and among the many stories that she tells,

00:42:57.270 --> 00:43:00.090
she tells one in particular
of a Nashville man

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:02.960
named Pat Hanifan, who had
outraged a little colored girl.

00:43:02.960 --> 00:43:06.930
And for his crime, he was in
fact sentenced to six months

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:11.800
in the city jail, but upon
release became a city detective.

00:43:11.800 --> 00:43:16.800
And this is not just our
2021 sense of outrage

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:21.850
at the hypocrisy in this, the
stories in her book in 1892,

00:43:21.850 --> 00:43:26.240
precisely because she understood
it as outrageous, as well.

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:28.750
Nathaniel Shaler, who was a self-described

00:43:28.750 --> 00:43:31.710
friend of the negro, a liberal at the time

00:43:31.710 --> 00:43:35.380
who essentially asked this
statistical question in 1890,

00:43:35.380 --> 00:43:38.390
which said a lot of this
motion, a decade later

00:43:38.390 --> 00:43:40.940
starts to evaluate the
evidence that's come in,

00:43:40.940 --> 00:43:44.100
and he takes a much more
centrist-moderate view,

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:46.540
that perhaps even out of
the work of Ida B. Wells,

00:43:46.540 --> 00:43:48.470
he was inclined to
believe that the problem

00:43:48.470 --> 00:43:50.580
of rape on the whole was more problem

00:43:50.580 --> 00:43:52.270
of white male rape than black male rape,

00:43:52.270 --> 00:43:54.040
noting there is less
danger to be apprehended

00:43:54.040 --> 00:43:56.120
from them in this regard
than from an equal body

00:43:56.120 --> 00:43:57.870
of whites of the like social grade.

00:43:59.090 --> 00:44:02.820
And so, within this
constellation, I want to turn

00:44:02.820 --> 00:44:06.450
our attention to, for a few
minutes, how this moves.

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:08.440
So, this is material that comes

00:44:08.440 --> 00:44:11.346
from chapter three,
not out of chapter two,

00:44:11.346 --> 00:44:16.346
because the continuation of
the story of Hoffman's impact

00:44:16.890 --> 00:44:20.860
on setting the terms of how to
judge black people's fitness

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:24.880
for citizenship is also
born out by the legacy

00:44:24.880 --> 00:44:28.420
of Hoffman and others in
pointing to European immigrants

00:44:28.420 --> 00:44:31.570
and low-income white people as
being worthy of citizenship,

00:44:31.570 --> 00:44:34.750
because their crime was not disqualifying.

00:44:34.750 --> 00:44:38.550
And in this way, studies
like this, Frederick Bushee

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:41.210
on Ethnic Factors in Population of Boston

00:44:41.210 --> 00:44:45.110
highlight the criminality
of the Irish and Italians.

00:44:45.110 --> 00:44:47.230
There's a moral degradation
among Irish families.

00:44:47.230 --> 00:44:48.650
For quarrels which are a serious affair,

00:44:48.650 --> 00:44:50.390
for flashes of anger,
which mean a knife thrust,

00:44:50.390 --> 00:44:52.860
must one go to the Italian quarters.

00:44:52.860 --> 00:44:56.200
These are both expressions of the kind

00:44:56.200 --> 00:45:00.170
of racialized logics that
apply to European immigrants,

00:45:00.170 --> 00:45:02.320
but these are arguments in favor,

00:45:02.320 --> 00:45:05.040
not a further criminalization,
but of assimilation,

00:45:05.040 --> 00:45:06.920
which is itself a form of violence.

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:09.200
But within this context, the point is not

00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:11.780
to be punitive, but to incorporate people

00:45:11.780 --> 00:45:14.560
through fairer distribution
of public goods.

00:45:14.560 --> 00:45:16.003
And so, people make the argument

00:45:16.003 --> 00:45:17.900
that these are Americans in process,

00:45:17.900 --> 00:45:20.350
these are fellow passengers
on our ship of state.

00:45:21.270 --> 00:45:23.740
Hoffman himself engaged on this point,

00:45:23.740 --> 00:45:26.410
but again, his agenda was
to single out black people

00:45:26.410 --> 00:45:30.230
as exceptional, by
comparison to the Irish,

00:45:30.230 --> 00:45:35.230
and exceptionally worthy of
divestment and not investment.

00:45:36.330 --> 00:45:38.550
Du Bois, as you know, from your reading,

00:45:38.550 --> 00:45:41.480
went to great lengths to
expose the hypocrisies

00:45:41.480 --> 00:45:43.250
and contradictions of Hoffman's work

00:45:43.250 --> 00:45:45.150
and the body of work that came from it.

00:45:45.150 --> 00:45:47.320
He was roundly and consistently dismissed

00:45:47.320 --> 00:45:49.773
as being biased towards his own people.

00:45:54.030 --> 00:45:55.430
Include University of Pennsylvania,

00:45:55.430 --> 00:45:58.130
sociologists saying that Du
Bois had a chip on his shoulder,

00:45:58.130 --> 00:46:00.260
because surely his
accomplishments were evidence

00:46:00.260 --> 00:46:03.520
not of white supremacy, but
of opportunity in the country.

00:46:03.520 --> 00:46:04.480
Pretty remarkable.

00:46:04.480 --> 00:46:07.670
And so, here Du Bois, who's
frustrated by a decade

00:46:07.670 --> 00:46:09.940
after writing The Philadelphia
Negro and an essay

00:46:09.940 --> 00:46:14.150
called The Souls of White
Folks, very expressively

00:46:14.150 --> 00:46:16.800
describes the circumstances
of this contradiction.

00:46:16.800 --> 00:46:19.380
Murder may swagger, theft may
rule, prostitution flourish,

00:46:19.380 --> 00:46:21.210
and the nation gives but spasmodic,

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:22.730
intermittent and lukewarm attention.

00:46:22.730 --> 00:46:25.290
But let the murder be
black or the thief brown,

00:46:25.290 --> 00:46:28.010
and the righteousness of
indignation sweeps the world.

00:46:28.010 --> 00:46:30.330
It is blackness that is
condemned, and not crime.

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:31.850
If it isn't obvious, that's where

00:46:31.850 --> 00:46:33.763
the title of the book comes from.

00:46:34.860 --> 00:46:36.470
Others looked at these contradictions

00:46:36.470 --> 00:46:38.730
and begin to give voice to them.

00:46:38.730 --> 00:46:41.260
Here's an editorial from the NAACP,

00:46:41.260 --> 00:46:44.000
pointing out, as I like
to say, the two men,

00:46:44.000 --> 00:46:47.730
the white man to the left
of the nattily-dressed man,

00:46:47.730 --> 00:46:49.880
as well as the black man to the right

00:46:49.880 --> 00:46:54.230
of his counterpart are our equivalent

00:46:54.230 --> 00:46:56.820
of people sagging in
the early 20th century.

00:46:56.820 --> 00:47:00.330
These are people who are
marked by a class identity

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:03.410
within their race, which
makes them suspect.

00:47:03.410 --> 00:47:05.860
And the point here in American logic

00:47:05.860 --> 00:47:08.560
is that white people see
this as individual failure,

00:47:08.560 --> 00:47:12.650
whereas American logic,
white people see a black man

00:47:12.650 --> 00:47:16.020
whose class position makes
him proximate to criminality,

00:47:16.020 --> 00:47:19.070
as evidence of the race, which
is racial criminalization.

00:47:19.070 --> 00:47:20.600
If they had that language at the time,

00:47:20.600 --> 00:47:22.790
that's what they would have called this.

00:47:22.790 --> 00:47:26.380
And so, that logic leads
to a predictable outcome.

00:47:26.380 --> 00:47:29.170
On the ground in the work of progressives,

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:32.230
so the set of ideas to justify
reading crime statistics

00:47:32.230 --> 00:47:35.390
is evidence of social economic
and political inequality

00:47:35.390 --> 00:47:37.430
then leads to arguments such as this,

00:47:37.430 --> 00:47:40.640
and this illustration from a
prisoner reentry organization

00:47:40.640 --> 00:47:45.020
in Chicago describes the scene of a mother

00:47:45.020 --> 00:47:47.670
raising her children potentially alone.

00:47:47.670 --> 00:47:50.420
It's not clear if her
husband is in prison,

00:47:50.420 --> 00:47:52.640
or down the street, or absent altogether.

00:47:52.640 --> 00:47:54.670
What we do see is that she has

00:47:54.670 --> 00:47:56.470
five kids, she's got a lot of babies.

00:47:56.470 --> 00:47:59.530
And so, within the context
of eugenic concerns,

00:47:59.530 --> 00:48:02.130
she shouldn't have had all
those babies in the first place.

00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:05.950
But for progressives, the
problem here is that she lives

00:48:05.950 --> 00:48:09.660
in a community of poor
people with broken windows.

00:48:09.660 --> 00:48:12.070
And that leads to juvenile delinquency,

00:48:12.070 --> 00:48:14.010
as two kids are fighting in the street.

00:48:14.010 --> 00:48:17.020
Every poor community or immigrant slum

00:48:17.020 --> 00:48:19.370
in the early 20th Century
in a place like Chicago

00:48:19.370 --> 00:48:22.010
also has some kind of saloon
or bar around the corner.

00:48:22.010 --> 00:48:23.040
It's hard to see in this image,

00:48:23.040 --> 00:48:25.170
but it's the sign here where my mouse is.

00:48:25.170 --> 00:48:27.440
And of course, every bad
neighborhood then and now

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:32.370
has some overpopulation
of police activity.

00:48:32.370 --> 00:48:36.280
And here, some cop is dragging
some guy off the street.

00:48:36.280 --> 00:48:38.640
But rather than make this a call about

00:48:38.640 --> 00:48:40.880
personal responsibility or chastity

00:48:40.880 --> 00:48:45.270
and morality, as Hoffman did,
these progresses in Chicago

00:48:45.270 --> 00:48:49.010
made this an issue about larger structure.

00:48:49.010 --> 00:48:50.950
So long as there are bad
tenements, sweatshops,

00:48:50.950 --> 00:48:52.950
brutal policemen, bad jails, child labor,

00:48:52.950 --> 00:48:55.330
dishonest and grinding employers,
saloons and gambling dens,

00:48:55.330 --> 00:48:56.800
so long as boys are taught to fight

00:48:56.800 --> 00:48:58.080
and allowed to carry firearms,

00:48:58.080 --> 00:48:59.630
so long as fathers are
indifferent deserters

00:48:59.630 --> 00:49:01.620
and mothers must maintain
the family by the washboard,

00:49:01.620 --> 00:49:03.220
so long crime will continue.

00:49:03.220 --> 00:49:06.020
What will you do to help this
association to prevent it?

00:49:07.015 --> 00:49:09.330
And this story plays
out time and time again.

00:49:09.330 --> 00:49:12.590
Jane Adams is the leading
social worker of the period.

00:49:12.590 --> 00:49:14.320
She has tremendous power and influence

00:49:14.320 --> 00:49:17.320
as a middle-class white
woman, social reformer.

00:49:17.320 --> 00:49:20.110
She writes several books, she redefines

00:49:20.110 --> 00:49:24.220
the problem of community
harm and juvenile delinquency

00:49:24.220 --> 00:49:29.220
and social dysfunction,
not just as a problem

00:49:29.280 --> 00:49:31.340
of society and the mistreatment of views,

00:49:31.340 --> 00:49:33.020
but also not as one that is attached

00:49:33.020 --> 00:49:37.130
to immigrant status or even
attached to nationality.

00:49:37.130 --> 00:49:39.810
She is decriminalizing and de-racializing

00:49:39.810 --> 00:49:41.810
the problem of white youth criminality,

00:49:41.810 --> 00:49:45.150
because the statistics she's
facing are overwhelming.

00:49:45.150 --> 00:49:48.530
15,000 youth brought before
the juvenile court in 1904,

00:49:48.530 --> 00:49:50.370
she's talking about white kids.

00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:52.270
She describes them as
stealing from their parents,

00:49:52.270 --> 00:49:55.770
swiping junk, pawning their
clothes and shoes, doing, quote,

00:49:55.770 --> 00:49:58.260
any desperate thing to get
the dope, as they called it.

00:49:58.260 --> 00:50:00.210
She describes daily acts of violence,

00:50:00.210 --> 00:50:02.810
of interpersonal violence,
as was true in the case

00:50:02.810 --> 00:50:07.500
of this example of people using firearms.

00:50:07.500 --> 00:50:10.300
So, the point is I'm showing
you the primary evidence

00:50:10.300 --> 00:50:13.950
upon which we can see, up
close, how this is playing out.

00:50:13.950 --> 00:50:16.754
And she also argues, so a Polish youth

00:50:16.754 --> 00:50:17.587
shot an Irish boy in the head.

00:50:17.587 --> 00:50:19.400
This tale could be duplicated
almost every morning.

00:50:19.400 --> 00:50:21.510
What might be merely a
boyish scrap is turned

00:50:21.510 --> 00:50:23.930
into a tragedy because
some boy has a revolver.

00:50:23.930 --> 00:50:25.860
And she adds, we certainly
cannot expect the fathers

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:27.640
and mothers who have come
to the city from farms

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:28.890
or have immigrated from other lands

00:50:28.890 --> 00:50:30.870
to appreciate or rectify these dangers,

00:50:30.870 --> 00:50:32.640
nor could we expect the
young people themselves

00:50:32.640 --> 00:50:35.256
to kill the cancer of
modern city conditions.

00:50:35.256 --> 00:50:38.400
Jane Adams is an entire corpus of work,

00:50:38.400 --> 00:50:40.850
and that corpus of work is also echoed

00:50:40.850 --> 00:50:44.830
by the University of Chicago
and its ecological approach,

00:50:44.830 --> 00:50:47.390
which saw immigrant
criminality and poverty,

00:50:47.390 --> 00:50:50.193
or immigrant crime and
poverty, as evidence,

00:50:55.728 --> 00:50:57.420
groups and the solution to
that was to disperse them

00:50:57.420 --> 00:50:58.980
into communities with greater opportunity

00:50:58.980 --> 00:51:02.340
to lift up their economic
base and to engage

00:51:02.340 --> 00:51:05.870
in various forms of police
reform and other things.

00:51:05.870 --> 00:51:07.910
Her work was praised for being

00:51:07.910 --> 00:51:12.020
even poetic, within the
bounds of sociology,

00:51:12.020 --> 00:51:14.850
which would be a rare thing today.

00:51:14.850 --> 00:51:17.820
But of course, people
do win Pulitzer prizes,

00:51:17.820 --> 00:51:19.350
which I guess is kind of the equivalent,

00:51:19.350 --> 00:51:21.350
for writing a great work of ethnography,

00:51:22.731 --> 00:51:24.760
such as Matt Desmond, so on and so forth.

00:51:24.760 --> 00:51:28.970
Just to illustrate very quickly,
these politics of knowledge

00:51:28.970 --> 00:51:30.980
and the violence of racial quantification

00:51:30.980 --> 00:51:35.980
are also showing up and
black conservative voices.

00:51:36.620 --> 00:51:39.023
Booker T. Washington is
one expression of this,

00:51:40.110 --> 00:51:41.720
perhaps not as harsh as this one.

00:51:41.720 --> 00:51:43.710
This is a man named
William Hannibal Thomas,

00:51:43.710 --> 00:51:46.410
who you also were introduced
to from your reading.

00:51:46.410 --> 00:51:49.920
He says that he's writing to
sociologists in the country

00:51:49.920 --> 00:51:52.580
because he has firsthand
experience of what he's seen,

00:51:52.580 --> 00:51:55.720
and as a black man, he
can testify with certainty

00:51:55.720 --> 00:51:59.250
that it is true, black
people are degrading

00:51:59.250 --> 00:52:03.330
into savage behavior,
they are savage at heart,

00:52:03.330 --> 00:52:05.790
unable, practically to discern
between right and wrong,

00:52:05.790 --> 00:52:07.550
intrinsically inferior type of humanity,

00:52:07.550 --> 00:52:09.200
a record of lawless existence led

00:52:09.200 --> 00:52:10.690
by every impulse and passion.

00:52:10.690 --> 00:52:12.950
Really, the inferiority of
the negro in mind, morals,

00:52:12.950 --> 00:52:15.510
judgment and character is
such that there is no doubt

00:52:15.510 --> 00:52:17.390
that some very plausible
confirmatory evidence

00:52:17.390 --> 00:52:19.800
of the justness of the
simian theory of human origin

00:52:19.800 --> 00:52:22.240
might be derived from a close
inspection of his demeanor.

00:52:22.240 --> 00:52:25.270
That's a mouthful, but he
basically said that he's convinced

00:52:25.270 --> 00:52:28.550
by people really are
closer to apes and monkeys

00:52:28.550 --> 00:52:30.690
than they are to human beings.

00:52:30.690 --> 00:52:34.130
Of course, he wrote this around
the same time as a young man

00:52:34.130 --> 00:52:37.360
named Ota Benga from the
Congo, literally placed

00:52:37.360 --> 00:52:42.360
in the Bronx Zoo on
display in the monkey cage.

00:52:42.390 --> 00:52:45.980
So, that Hannibal Thomas actually did not

00:52:45.980 --> 00:52:48.043
present the kind of statistical evidence

00:52:48.043 --> 00:52:51.110
that one could expect of such claims,

00:52:51.110 --> 00:52:53.120
in the way that both Hoffman used them,

00:52:53.120 --> 00:52:56.700
and even James K. Vardaman,
the Mississippi governor,

00:52:56.700 --> 00:53:01.310
is surprising, because one would
have expected that his book

00:53:01.310 --> 00:53:05.230
would have simply been ignored
by academics, but it wasn't.

00:53:05.230 --> 00:53:08.830
In fact, by Franklin Giddings,
a towering intellectual

00:53:08.830 --> 00:53:10.530
within the social science community,

00:53:10.530 --> 00:53:11.910
the founder of Columbia University's

00:53:11.910 --> 00:53:13.800
sociology department
and the third President

00:53:13.800 --> 00:53:15.590
of the American Sociological Association

00:53:15.590 --> 00:53:18.440
praised William Hannibal Thomas' book.

00:53:18.440 --> 00:53:20.900
As a sociological study, it
is one of the most valuable

00:53:20.900 --> 00:53:22.720
things to be put in the
hands of genuine students

00:53:22.720 --> 00:53:24.620
of American conditions that I know of,

00:53:24.620 --> 00:53:27.620
presents its subject without
an atom of the sentimentality,

00:53:27.620 --> 00:53:29.150
which is so often proved a blemish

00:53:29.150 --> 00:53:31.127
in many books otherwise most excellent.

00:53:31.127 --> 00:53:34.010
And so, we've just seen an
example where one is praised,

00:53:34.010 --> 00:53:36.540
in the case of Jane
Adams, for sentimentality,

00:53:36.540 --> 00:53:39.170
and yet Hoffman, a black man
who has written without any

00:53:39.170 --> 00:53:43.370
of the structural rules of
social science, is praised

00:53:43.370 --> 00:53:45.940
for being objective because
he is a member of the race.

00:53:45.940 --> 00:53:48.400
New York Times also gave it a rave review.

00:53:48.400 --> 00:53:53.400
And so, to close, the echo
of this moment leads to

00:53:54.800 --> 00:53:59.800
what will continue to be
various forms of redlining,

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:05.090
of instrumental violence,
of police control,

00:54:05.090 --> 00:54:08.070
and eventually police taking on the work

00:54:08.070 --> 00:54:12.930
of maintaining the second-class
and segregated lives

00:54:12.930 --> 00:54:14.510
of black people outside of the South.

00:54:14.510 --> 00:54:16.750
This is a scene from
the Chicago race riot.

00:54:16.750 --> 00:54:19.910
I won't dwell on it, we can
talk more about it in a Q&amp;A.

00:54:19.910 --> 00:54:21.720
Black people in defense of themselves

00:54:21.720 --> 00:54:25.860
are searched and stopped and disarmed.

00:54:25.860 --> 00:54:28.380
Out of that report came a
number of recommendations.

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:31.040
These recommendations, I
won't read in this instance,

00:54:31.040 --> 00:54:33.890
other than to say that they recommended,

00:54:33.890 --> 00:54:36.410
led by a black sociologist
from the University of Chicago,

00:54:36.410 --> 00:54:37.840
that crime statistics themselves

00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:40.390
ought to be jettisoned,
because they were overly biased

00:54:40.390 --> 00:54:43.910
and we're giving bad or false information,

00:54:43.910 --> 00:54:46.370
evidence of their inaccuracy
and misrepresentation.

00:54:46.370 --> 00:54:49.900
And the leading criminologists
agreed in two editions

00:54:49.900 --> 00:54:52.523
that these statistics were
completely unreliable.

00:54:54.280 --> 00:54:55.690
I'm going to skip over this example

00:54:55.690 --> 00:54:57.700
in the interest of time,
we can come back to it.

00:54:57.700 --> 00:54:59.340
So, we could ask the
question, what happened

00:54:59.340 --> 00:55:01.820
to Bushee's Italian and Irish criminals?

00:55:01.820 --> 00:55:04.050
How many Titan Americans
committed armed robbery last year,

00:55:04.050 --> 00:55:09.050
Irish American burglars, or
in this case, we can't know.

00:55:10.150 --> 00:55:12.550
The answer is that those
statistics disappeared.

00:55:13.800 --> 00:55:17.670
The fact of their disappearance
is the evidence of the way

00:55:17.670 --> 00:55:20.480
that this use of crime statistics,

00:55:20.480 --> 00:55:23.993
as Professor Stanley would
describe as a former propaganda,

00:55:25.420 --> 00:55:28.050
continues to drive discussion and debate

00:55:28.050 --> 00:55:30.990
about the fate and future of black people.

00:55:30.990 --> 00:55:33.310
Edwin Sutherland noted
that one of the reasons

00:55:33.310 --> 00:55:36.010
why they stopped counting the
Irish and Italian Americans

00:55:36.010 --> 00:55:38.930
is because they appear
to approach the same kind

00:55:38.930 --> 00:55:41.890
of behavior as white
Americans, native born,

00:55:41.890 --> 00:55:45.580
which is to essentially
say, as long as black people

00:55:45.580 --> 00:55:48.240
could be singled out, or
Mexicans, or Chinese-Americans

00:55:48.240 --> 00:55:51.040
or Chinese immigrants, for
having excessive crime rates,

00:55:56.450 --> 00:55:58.390
and just to show you, this is crystallized

00:55:58.390 --> 00:56:00.330
in Uniform Crime Reports,
which to this day

00:56:00.330 --> 00:56:03.160
are the most authoritative
form of arrest statistics

00:56:03.160 --> 00:56:05.350
as an indices of crime in the nation.

00:56:05.350 --> 00:56:07.720
In 1933, when they began,
they pointed out negroes

00:56:07.720 --> 00:56:09.570
and the foreign born should be watched.

00:56:09.570 --> 00:56:11.500
By 1940, it is significant to point out

00:56:11.500 --> 00:56:13.030
that the figure for native whites includes

00:56:13.030 --> 00:56:15.530
the immediate descendants
of foreign born individuals.

00:56:15.530 --> 00:56:17.880
This is a form of statistical white flight

00:56:17.880 --> 00:56:20.620
at precisely the moment
where white flight is,

00:56:20.620 --> 00:56:24.360
in fact, beginning to
happen in parts the country.

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:26.740
The Kerner Commission picks
up on the same recommendations

00:56:26.740 --> 00:56:29.620
as the Chicago report,
which is why I didn't

00:56:29.620 --> 00:56:30.920
read those in interest of time,

00:56:30.920 --> 00:56:33.720
but they essentially
amount to better treatment

00:56:33.720 --> 00:56:36.040
of citizens, more police protection,

00:56:36.040 --> 00:56:38.180
because one of the other sides of the coin

00:56:38.180 --> 00:56:40.900
of racist policing is
also under-protection.

00:56:40.900 --> 00:56:43.040
They call for independent
citizen review boards,

00:56:43.040 --> 00:56:45.710
a stop to aggressive patrol,
develop community policing.

00:56:45.710 --> 00:56:48.290
A lot of this should sound familiar,

00:56:48.290 --> 00:56:51.080
so familiar that even for Kenneth Clark,

00:56:51.080 --> 00:56:53.100
who was famous for
having done doll studies

00:56:53.100 --> 00:56:55.320
and contributing to the
end of formal segregation

00:56:55.320 --> 00:56:57.570
in the '54 Supreme court decision

00:56:57.570 --> 00:56:59.020
on Brown vs Board of Education,

00:56:59.020 --> 00:57:00.970
notes that he'd read
the Chicago Riot report,

00:57:00.970 --> 00:57:05.140
he read another one in Harlem
in 1935, another one in 1943,

00:57:05.140 --> 00:57:08.670
and added that he kept reading
the same recommendations

00:57:08.670 --> 00:57:10.810
and that it must be a kind
of Alice in Wonderland

00:57:10.810 --> 00:57:13.120
with the same moving picture
we shown over and over again,

00:57:13.120 --> 00:57:16.610
same analysis, same
recommendations and same inaction.

00:57:16.610 --> 00:57:20.210
And so, here we are with
Bloomberg and Compstat.

00:57:20.210 --> 00:57:23.030
The over-representation
of black and Latinos

00:57:23.030 --> 00:57:26.130
relative to white in the
era of mass incarceration,

00:57:26.130 --> 00:57:28.350
the ongoing use of violent crime rates.

00:57:28.350 --> 00:57:29.600
The notion here is that because

00:57:29.600 --> 00:57:32.670
crime dropped, incarceration must work.

00:57:32.670 --> 00:57:34.800
You see an inverse relationship
with stop and frisk.

00:57:34.800 --> 00:57:38.660
This was the defense of stop
and frisk as a massive system

00:57:38.660 --> 00:57:41.460
of racial control and
police discrimination

00:57:41.460 --> 00:57:44.840
that was ultimately ruled as such in 2013.

00:57:44.840 --> 00:57:46.740
And what I wanted to show you
here is what collective guilt

00:57:46.740 --> 00:57:49.420
looks like, because the long
legacy of Frederick Douglas

00:57:49.420 --> 00:57:52.440
is articulated by the
former police commissioner

00:57:52.440 --> 00:57:55.980
in New York, Ray Kelly,
who knows it doesn't mean

00:57:55.980 --> 00:57:57.610
that people are not doing anything wrong

00:57:57.610 --> 00:57:59.530
to the question of stopping
first racial profiling.

00:57:59.530 --> 00:58:01.770
If you look at the statute,
it says reasonable suspicion

00:58:01.770 --> 00:58:03.900
that individuals might be
about to commit a crime.

00:58:03.900 --> 00:58:05.190
As a preventative aspect of this,

00:58:05.190 --> 00:58:07.790
people say innocent, that
is not the appropriate word.

00:58:07.790 --> 00:58:09.320
That's what collective guilt looks like.

00:58:09.320 --> 00:58:10.690
And then he notes, this is standard

00:58:10.690 --> 00:58:12.690
law enforcement practice around America.

00:58:14.040 --> 00:58:16.050
I won't dwell on this, but this comes back

00:58:16.050 --> 00:58:19.630
to the same kinds of
infrastructure of criminality

00:58:19.630 --> 00:58:21.750
that we saw in Kelly Lytle's work,

00:58:21.750 --> 00:58:25.290
drunkenness, disorderly
conduct, public urination.

00:58:25.290 --> 00:58:29.520
The scale of Latino and black
stops produced little more

00:58:29.520 --> 00:58:31.310
than summonses when people were stopped,

00:58:31.310 --> 00:58:35.020
but 90% weren't even issued
so much as summonses,

00:58:35.020 --> 00:58:36.820
because the vast majority
of people were stopped

00:58:36.820 --> 00:58:39.143
for things that were not, in fact, crimes.

00:58:40.180 --> 00:58:41.970
Ferguson had the same problem.

00:58:41.970 --> 00:58:43.470
We know from the result, they resorted

00:58:43.470 --> 00:58:45.550
to racist ideas to justify it.

00:58:45.550 --> 00:58:47.360
And so, I'm reminded when we go back

00:58:47.360 --> 00:58:49.770
to the racial terror era of lynching

00:58:49.770 --> 00:58:52.440
that when we look at
police consent decrees,

00:58:52.440 --> 00:58:54.740
since 1995, there were about 70 of them,

00:58:54.740 --> 00:58:58.220
this is just a partial list,
it kind of looks the same,

00:58:58.220 --> 00:59:00.410
It looks like how we might
look back on this moment

00:59:00.410 --> 00:59:04.580
and see the same tally of state violence

00:59:04.580 --> 00:59:06.840
in this moment as
similar to state violence

00:59:06.840 --> 00:59:08.090
in the lynching era, which, of course,

00:59:08.090 --> 00:59:10.070
in the lynching era was defensible.

00:59:10.070 --> 00:59:11.750
And yet we're still having a debate

00:59:11.750 --> 00:59:14.683
about the defensibility of
state violence in this moment.

00:59:16.550 --> 00:59:19.040
And so, I close with one final echo,

00:59:19.040 --> 00:59:22.950
and that echo is that we
could read the evidence

00:59:22.950 --> 00:59:26.100
of opioid deaths in
the nation in this kind

00:59:26.100 --> 00:59:28.030
of data visualization
that the New York Times

00:59:28.030 --> 00:59:33.030
did a few years ago with the
evidence of wide-scale death.

00:59:33.420 --> 00:59:36.760
Not too much of a leap to
note that that death itself

00:59:36.760 --> 00:59:39.750
is wrapped up in various forms
of illegal drug consumption

00:59:39.750 --> 00:59:43.240
and distribution and related
crimes that go along with it.

00:59:43.240 --> 00:59:46.200
And yet, we are to understand as a society

00:59:46.200 --> 00:59:49.290
that this tally of death in white America

00:59:49.290 --> 00:59:51.280
is evidence of a public health challenge.

00:59:51.280 --> 00:59:54.200
And Chris Christie articulates
this quite clearly,

00:59:54.200 --> 00:59:55.670
addiction is an illness and we can beat.

00:59:55.670 --> 00:59:57.120
If we give the people
the tools and support,

00:59:57.120 --> 00:59:58.450
they need to overcome this disease.

00:59:58.450 --> 01:00:00.570
If we choose to free people
from the stigma of addiction,

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:01.910
and recognize it as the
public health challenge

01:00:01.910 --> 01:00:04.640
it truly is, we can help
people to reclaim their lives.

01:00:04.640 --> 01:00:07.820
Think a great army of unfortunates.

01:00:07.820 --> 01:00:09.640
We can find the true
measure of our compassion.

01:00:09.640 --> 01:00:11.210
The investment we're
making will change lives

01:00:11.210 --> 01:00:13.320
and get more people into treatment earlier

01:00:13.320 --> 01:00:15.990
instead of the emergency
room or prison later.

01:00:15.990 --> 01:00:19.280
So, this is a call for
disrupting the social conditions

01:00:19.280 --> 01:00:21.860
leading to premature
death in white America.

01:00:21.860 --> 01:00:25.780
And with that, thank you so
much for following that along.

01:00:25.780 --> 01:00:27.430
Look forward to the conversation.

01:00:33.200 --> 01:00:36.450
- Thank you so much Khalil,
and I loved you're adding

01:00:36.450 --> 01:00:39.529
New York Times also gave it a rave review.

01:00:39.529 --> 01:00:41.060
(men laughing)

01:00:41.060 --> 01:00:42.363
History does not change.

01:00:46.524 --> 01:00:47.730
So, first of all, I want to say,

01:00:47.730 --> 01:00:50.923
everyone place your
questions into the Q&amp;A,

01:00:52.656 --> 01:00:56.440
and I will start collecting the questions

01:00:56.440 --> 01:01:00.053
and we'll moderate them
as they start to roll in.

01:01:01.060 --> 01:01:05.620
So, let's begin.

01:01:05.620 --> 01:01:06.743
Tim, do you have?

01:01:07.710 --> 01:01:09.820
- I mean, I had a thought, Khalil,

01:01:09.820 --> 01:01:10.890
that I'd like to share with you

01:01:10.890 --> 01:01:15.890
and see if you've had a similar thought.

01:01:15.900 --> 01:01:19.340
In your book, you raise the
tantalizing possibility,

01:01:19.340 --> 01:01:22.220
which we can't know
whether Frederick Hoffman

01:01:22.220 --> 01:01:25.160
had red Ida B. Wells, and whether,

01:01:25.160 --> 01:01:27.880
in some sense he was responding to her.

01:01:27.880 --> 01:01:30.770
And in reading these two primary readings,

01:01:30.770 --> 01:01:33.160
which Jason assigned along with your book,

01:01:33.160 --> 01:01:38.160
I was struck by an really
strange structural similarity

01:01:40.600 --> 01:01:44.710
that led me to think of
what Hoffman was doing

01:01:44.710 --> 01:01:48.520
as a kind of scientific lynching.

01:01:48.520 --> 01:01:50.810
And the reason I'm thinking about this

01:01:50.810 --> 01:01:54.140
are two elements that I
draw from Ida B. Wells.

01:01:54.140 --> 01:01:59.140
The first is, she points out
that what a lynch mob is doing

01:02:00.150 --> 01:02:02.783
is basically discrediting the state.

01:02:03.810 --> 01:02:07.363
A lynch mob is saying, these
people don't deserve the law.

01:02:09.150 --> 01:02:10.530
By saying that, you're basically

01:02:10.530 --> 01:02:12.360
pushing the state out of the picture.

01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:13.440
You're pushing the rule of law

01:02:13.440 --> 01:02:15.550
out of the picture in
the conventional sense.

01:02:15.550 --> 01:02:17.130
And the other things she's doing,

01:02:17.130 --> 01:02:20.370
which seems rather
courageous, or was courageous,

01:02:20.370 --> 01:02:22.640
is that she's drawing
attention to the gender,

01:02:22.640 --> 01:02:26.310
or the sexual aspect of it, right?

01:02:26.310 --> 01:02:31.200
And then, if you read
the whole Hoffman thing,

01:02:31.200 --> 01:02:36.140
you can look at what he's
doing as the same thing

01:02:36.140 --> 01:02:39.283
that she's criticizing,
but on a much more,

01:02:40.160 --> 01:02:43.160
in this empiricist way,
in a scholarly way.

01:02:43.160 --> 01:02:47.780
Because he's arguing a lynch
mob would say one black man

01:02:47.780 --> 01:02:52.030
did something on the basis of
hearsay or rumor, or whatever.

01:02:52.030 --> 01:02:57.030
And he's saying the whole
black race does things, right?

01:02:57.060 --> 01:02:59.470
And he's doing it on the
basis of this sketchy reading

01:02:59.470 --> 01:03:02.510
of evidence, but it's the same mood.

01:03:02.510 --> 01:03:04.020
And then, there's the same conclusion,

01:03:04.020 --> 01:03:07.700
which is that these people
basically don't deserve the law.

01:03:07.700 --> 01:03:09.190
They're, outside the law.

01:03:09.190 --> 01:03:11.407
Maybe the state should act
to protect other people,

01:03:11.407 --> 01:03:13.540
but the state shouldn't
act to protect them.

01:03:13.540 --> 01:03:16.710
And there's this weird
dichotomy, which you emphasize

01:03:16.710 --> 01:03:20.290
in your talk, where
Hoffman is basically saying

01:03:20.290 --> 01:03:22.170
the state should be giving white people

01:03:22.170 --> 01:03:23.550
good conditions for existence.

01:03:23.550 --> 01:03:27.203
Meanwhile, it should be social
Darwinism for black people.

01:03:28.380 --> 01:03:29.480
This funny thing.

01:03:29.480 --> 01:03:32.262
But then there's also this
gender component, too.

01:03:32.262 --> 01:03:35.680
Ida B. Wells is hinting, not hinting,

01:03:35.680 --> 01:03:37.730
she's suggesting pretty
openly, that there's

01:03:37.730 --> 01:03:40.223
an element of white male
anxiety about all this.

01:03:42.420 --> 01:03:44.290
And Hoffman has an answer to that.

01:03:44.290 --> 01:03:46.490
He's saying, look,
they're going to die out.

01:03:49.630 --> 01:03:50.880
They're going to die out.

01:03:52.160 --> 01:03:53.720
And when he was saying
that, I was thinking

01:03:53.720 --> 01:03:57.403
of some of the nastier
performative parts of lynching.

01:03:58.336 --> 01:04:01.900
When you say that a race is gonna die out,

01:04:01.900 --> 01:04:04.614
you're also going, that's
an argument about manhood.

01:04:04.614 --> 01:04:08.040
They can't keep it going,
they're gonna die out.

01:04:08.040 --> 01:04:09.420
So, reading these things together,

01:04:09.420 --> 01:04:12.320
along with your marvelous
work, I was struck

01:04:12.320 --> 01:04:15.028
by this kind of odd structural similarity

01:04:15.028 --> 01:04:19.730
between the way Ida B.
Wells describes lynching,

01:04:19.730 --> 01:04:23.350
and then the way that Hoffman
goes about describing crime.

01:04:23.350 --> 01:04:28.350
- Well, I think that's one of
the things that is surprising

01:04:28.480 --> 01:04:31.840
in examining Du Bois' work, as well.

01:04:31.840 --> 01:04:36.780
Du Bois is stuck trying
to essentially argue

01:04:37.890 --> 01:04:40.610
against racialization by using the same

01:04:40.610 --> 01:04:43.203
disparity data that
everybody else is using.

01:04:44.250 --> 01:04:49.180
He doesn't have any more
evidence than the same sets

01:04:49.180 --> 01:04:53.290
of quote-unquote facts about
disproportionate arrest rates

01:04:53.290 --> 01:04:56.220
in the city of Philadelphia, for example,

01:04:56.220 --> 01:05:00.100
except he has a different
interpretive lens

01:05:00.100 --> 01:05:04.060
for how to we explain what's
happening to black people.

01:05:04.060 --> 01:05:07.260
So, your point about the symmetry
with which these arguments

01:05:07.260 --> 01:05:12.260
are constructed is partly why
this period is so important,

01:05:15.720 --> 01:05:19.720
because we're stuck with the same form

01:05:19.720 --> 01:05:23.040
of the argument about what to make

01:05:23.040 --> 01:05:27.830
of what I would call statistical artifact.

01:05:27.830 --> 01:05:32.610
A statistical artifact is a
trace of a set of interactions,

01:05:32.610 --> 01:05:36.790
a set of power relationships,
a set of events,

01:05:36.790 --> 01:05:40.999
and what to make of those
events, as you point out rightly,

01:05:40.999 --> 01:05:44.311
Ida B. Wells is seeing
all sorts of things,

01:05:44.311 --> 01:05:47.480
as you point out, white
male anxiety, she's seeing

01:05:47.480 --> 01:05:50.580
different forms of victimization
on different scales.

01:05:50.580 --> 01:05:52.403
But essentially it's this,

01:05:56.410 --> 01:05:59.363
which is the evidence of
either a lynching or a rape

01:05:59.363 --> 01:06:02.993
that that is the linchpin
of the entire discussion.

01:06:08.430 --> 01:06:10.010
- I have a bunch of questions,

01:06:10.010 --> 01:06:13.593
I'm going to try to hold them tight.

01:06:17.141 --> 01:06:18.140
Let me see where to begin.

01:06:18.140 --> 01:06:22.290
So, first of all, do I have it right

01:06:22.290 --> 01:06:24.380
from the understanding of
the policing literature,

01:06:24.380 --> 01:06:26.980
that one of the things
they did in response

01:06:26.980 --> 01:06:29.200
to Irish and Italian American crime

01:06:29.200 --> 01:06:31.903
was to make Irish and
Italian Americans police?

01:06:34.390 --> 01:06:36.030
- That's one way to put it.

01:06:36.030 --> 01:06:38.760
I think the motivation wasn't
to solve the crime problem.

01:06:38.760 --> 01:06:41.043
The motivation was about image.

01:06:42.050 --> 01:06:45.140
It was a form of
distributing public goods,

01:06:45.140 --> 01:06:47.193
and in this case, civil service jobs.

01:06:48.985 --> 01:06:53.043
Let me just qualify that
with one additional note.

01:06:54.050 --> 01:06:58.250
In all of these groups, in these
liminal transitional spaces

01:06:58.250 --> 01:06:59.880
between being the target of policing

01:06:59.880 --> 01:07:02.750
to being part of the
police was usually a moment

01:07:02.750 --> 01:07:07.750
when some Irish guy gets
hired to then go be,

01:07:09.959 --> 01:07:10.792
(Khalil chuckling)

01:07:10.792 --> 01:07:15.130
the special forces guy for
rooting out Irish crime,

01:07:15.130 --> 01:07:17.930
or the Italian guy who does the same.

01:07:17.930 --> 01:07:19.963
These histories are really fascinating.

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:23.880
I've forgotten the guy who
was the first Italian-American

01:07:23.880 --> 01:07:27.450
who infiltrated the Sicilian
mob in New York City.

01:07:27.450 --> 01:07:29.780
And then, there's Samuel
Battle, who was the first

01:07:29.780 --> 01:07:32.230
black person who's assigned to Harlem.

01:07:32.230 --> 01:07:35.530
So, there's a little bit of
truth to what you're saying,

01:07:35.530 --> 01:07:37.680
but I think at scale, it was more about

01:07:37.680 --> 01:07:39.060
distributing civil service jobs,

01:07:39.060 --> 01:07:40.520
in which policing was one part of it.

01:07:40.520 --> 01:07:43.370
- But did they do something
similar with black Americans?

01:07:44.561 --> 01:07:46.463
Did make them into police?

01:07:49.682 --> 01:07:52.560
- Yeah, this is why I
answered your question

01:07:52.560 --> 01:07:55.100
about political patronage
and opportunity structures

01:07:55.100 --> 01:07:57.050
rather than about crime control,

01:07:57.050 --> 01:08:00.260
because in the case of
Italians and the Irish,

01:08:00.260 --> 01:08:03.963
they moved into these positions,
what I'd say, at scale,

01:08:05.140 --> 01:08:06.710
whereas for black people, they didn't

01:08:06.710 --> 01:08:08.960
move into these positions at scale.

01:08:08.960 --> 01:08:12.310
They were tokens, they were a little bit

01:08:12.310 --> 01:08:16.360
of inside intelligence for
helping to certain crimes.

01:08:16.360 --> 01:08:20.410
But in some ways, they were
a tiny sliver of patronage.

01:08:20.410 --> 01:08:25.040
So, we never saw the same
upward mobility as a form

01:08:25.040 --> 01:08:27.320
of political patronage for black people

01:08:27.320 --> 01:08:28.890
that would have positioned them to simply

01:08:28.890 --> 01:08:31.050
be the last immigrants to join the force,

01:08:31.050 --> 01:08:33.290
if we're going to use that metaphor.

01:08:33.290 --> 01:08:34.591
- Great.

01:08:34.591 --> 01:08:38.293
Okay, so two more quick questions.

01:08:41.020 --> 01:08:45.230
So one, I just want to check
by my teaching of Hoffman.

01:08:45.230 --> 01:08:49.030
I regularly teach Hoffman,
just because I think

01:08:49.030 --> 01:08:51.900
it probably holds the same
fascination for all of us,

01:08:51.900 --> 01:08:56.900
the way the he believes he's
objective, yet it's so clearly,

01:08:57.720 --> 01:09:02.720
you know, it's helpful
that Hoffman sometimes

01:09:03.020 --> 01:09:04.580
to remind yourself he's arguing

01:09:04.580 --> 01:09:06.760
that black people have less vital force

01:09:06.760 --> 01:09:09.060
than white people, and
you have to remember,

01:09:09.060 --> 01:09:11.273
there's no such thing as vital force.

01:09:11.273 --> 01:09:13.898
(Jason chuckling)

01:09:13.898 --> 01:09:16.150
So, but this massive
statistic, what you have,

01:09:16.150 --> 01:09:19.330
the core of so many hot
department's arguments

01:09:19.330 --> 01:09:23.090
are these claims that, in this
context, there's no racism.

01:09:23.090 --> 01:09:26.280
So, he says, look in the North,
we know there's no racism.

01:09:26.280 --> 01:09:28.900
In the South there's
racism, but in the North,

01:09:28.900 --> 01:09:30.500
we still have these disparities.

01:09:31.985 --> 01:09:35.540
So therefore, these
arguments of, oh, well,

01:09:35.540 --> 01:09:37.840
this is a group that
doesn't have black people,

01:09:37.840 --> 01:09:39.910
it doesn't face anti-black racism,

01:09:39.910 --> 01:09:44.203
that seems, to me, core
to racist arguments,

01:09:45.050 --> 01:09:47.963
not seeing the effects of racism,

01:09:49.433 --> 01:09:52.930
Now, with the disparity and COVID deaths,

01:09:52.930 --> 01:09:54.680
Hoffman would quickly conclude,

01:09:54.680 --> 01:09:56.693
oh, black people have less vital force.

01:09:57.870 --> 01:10:00.090
But now, people don't make that error.

01:10:00.090 --> 01:10:02.193
Like, well, clearly that's environmental.

01:10:03.610 --> 01:10:06.610
- Well, there are still medical
scientists and researchers

01:10:06.610 --> 01:10:08.880
who are literally still searching

01:10:08.880 --> 01:10:13.340
for the last remnants
of DNA that might reveal

01:10:13.340 --> 01:10:16.643
the fundamental differences
in health outcomes.

01:10:17.950 --> 01:10:21.440
The colloquial version of this
is that we're 99% the same,

01:10:21.440 --> 01:10:23.340
and some people might say 99.9%.

01:10:23.340 --> 01:10:26.660
But damn it, if that 0.1%
won't tell the whole story.

01:10:26.660 --> 01:10:28.123
That's kind of how it works.

01:10:29.050 --> 01:10:32.903
- And we see the same
exact thing with IQ debate.

01:10:35.037 --> 01:10:39.490
People say, oh, black kids
adopted into white families

01:10:40.390 --> 01:10:43.560
eventually do have an IQ deficit,

01:10:43.560 --> 01:10:48.560
but they don't face racism,
because the assumption that,

01:10:48.770 --> 01:10:52.370
(Jason chuckling)

01:10:52.370 --> 01:10:54.920
So, there's an echoing
of Hoffman's arguments

01:10:57.420 --> 01:10:59.466
I was wondering if you--

01:10:59.466 --> 01:11:01.400
- Yeah, I would just,

01:11:01.400 --> 01:11:02.233
- Yeah, go on.

01:11:03.910 --> 01:11:05.163
- Yeah. I was just going
to say, one of the things

01:11:05.163 --> 01:11:07.067
that as a writer I tried to do,

01:11:07.067 --> 01:11:09.730
and this might be a curiosity to students.

01:11:09.730 --> 01:11:14.574
I wrote about Hoffman
as someone who couldn't,

01:11:14.574 --> 01:11:15.990
you know, my job wasn't to pass judgment

01:11:15.990 --> 01:11:19.810
on Hoffman in analyzing his texts.

01:11:19.810 --> 01:11:22.877
And one of the ways that
I focused on doing that

01:11:22.877 --> 01:11:25.760
was to write from the perspective of him

01:11:25.760 --> 01:11:28.680
looking forward, rather
than us looking backwards.

01:11:28.680 --> 01:11:31.880
And I gave him a lot of
credit for his ingenuity

01:11:31.880 --> 01:11:36.280
and innovation and his
willingness to defend

01:11:36.280 --> 01:11:38.730
his point of view and to
search for the evidence,

01:11:38.730 --> 01:11:40.683
wherever it led him in could find it.

01:11:41.570 --> 01:11:45.090
And in a way, that's the
Du Bois' project also,

01:11:45.090 --> 01:11:47.546
just to come back to Tim's earlier point.

01:11:47.546 --> 01:11:50.500
Du Bois leaves Philadelphia
and goes to Atlanta

01:11:50.500 --> 01:11:53.900
and spends a decade
turning over every stone

01:11:53.900 --> 01:11:56.920
he can to take seriously,
maybe there is some evidence

01:11:56.920 --> 01:11:58.980
that black people have a
particular predisposition

01:11:58.980 --> 01:12:01.390
to various kinds of pathologies.

01:12:01.390 --> 01:12:04.500
He doesn't find it, and he gives up,

01:12:04.500 --> 01:12:05.820
and the only reason he gives up

01:12:05.820 --> 01:12:08.110
is because the deck's stacked against him.

01:12:08.110 --> 01:12:10.180
His arguments don't sit within a milieu

01:12:11.090 --> 01:12:13.660
that is inclined to read any form

01:12:13.660 --> 01:12:15.820
of black disadvantage of failure or death

01:12:15.820 --> 01:12:18.830
as evidence that there's
something wrong with black people.

01:12:18.830 --> 01:12:22.920
So to your point, the climate
in which academic research

01:12:22.920 --> 01:12:26.510
and various debates about
racial disparities today

01:12:26.510 --> 01:12:28.823
animate a lot of academic research,

01:12:29.680 --> 01:12:32.100
and it tends to be a little
bit more disciplinary

01:12:32.100 --> 01:12:33.790
in terms of who's on what side.

01:12:33.790 --> 01:12:37.730
Economists tend to be more on
the side of individual agency.

01:12:37.730 --> 01:12:40.290
It's a science of individual
in rational choice,

01:12:40.290 --> 01:12:42.600
so that's not entirely a surprise.

01:12:42.600 --> 01:12:45.290
And sociologists tend to be more inclined

01:12:45.290 --> 01:12:46.670
to take the structural argument.

01:12:46.670 --> 01:12:49.650
But there's heterogeneity in both sides

01:12:49.650 --> 01:12:51.241
and sociologists weren't always as open

01:12:51.241 --> 01:12:54.013
to these exogenous arguments
about black people.

01:12:56.170 --> 01:12:58.030
- I'm gonna do a followup
quickly and then hand it over

01:12:58.030 --> 01:13:00.230
to Tim, and then we'll go to questions.

01:13:00.230 --> 01:13:03.130
That's a great point,
because it brings out

01:13:07.900 --> 01:13:09.670
one of the things that's most criticized

01:13:09.670 --> 01:13:13.200
in the current anti-critical
race theory, moral panic,

01:13:13.200 --> 01:13:16.100
which is you're impugning objectivity.

01:13:16.100 --> 01:13:18.010
And you've just brought out brilliantly,

01:13:18.010 --> 01:13:21.550
I once I once had a Facebook
argument with Glenn Lowry,

01:13:21.550 --> 01:13:24.530
who was like falling into this, oh, well,

01:13:24.530 --> 01:13:26.230
there's objectivity and neutrality.

01:13:26.230 --> 01:13:28.440
And I was like, wait, so,

01:13:28.440 --> 01:13:30.853
economics doesn't have an ideology?

01:13:32.024 --> 01:13:33.870
And he was like, oh
yeah, okay, you're right.

01:13:33.870 --> 01:13:38.870
What a discipline is an ideology,
as you just pointed out.

01:13:40.696 --> 01:13:44.352
But this is all being what's happening.

01:13:44.352 --> 01:13:48.130
This point that any statistics,

01:13:48.130 --> 01:13:50.099
any interpretation of statistics,

01:13:50.099 --> 01:13:55.099
is going to be due through
an ideological lens

01:13:55.340 --> 01:14:00.340
is being attacked as an attack
on science and objectivity.

01:14:00.550 --> 01:14:01.810
- [Khalil] That's right, yeah.

01:14:01.810 --> 01:14:02.770
- Tim?

01:14:02.770 --> 01:14:05.050
- I mean, there's a dialectic
there, which is similar

01:14:05.050 --> 01:14:07.750
to the dialectic I wanted
to mention before we turn,

01:14:07.750 --> 01:14:10.870
and the dialectic is if you believe

01:14:10.870 --> 01:14:13.570
that you're the kind of
person who's always objective,

01:14:14.770 --> 01:14:17.710
that's a very deep form of subjectivity.

01:14:17.710 --> 01:14:19.750
- Right, exactly, yes.

01:14:19.750 --> 01:14:22.240
- There are a lot of folks
in these debates who think

01:14:22.240 --> 01:14:24.570
that they're the kind of people
who are always objective.

01:14:24.570 --> 01:14:25.440
- [Khalil] That's right.

01:14:25.440 --> 01:14:28.150
- Which is a marker of their subjectivity.

01:14:28.150 --> 01:14:30.470
I wanted to say something
similar about the law,

01:14:30.470 --> 01:14:33.970
because I was really
struck in your presentation

01:14:33.970 --> 01:14:36.250
and the way you went back and
forth between the sources,

01:14:36.250 --> 01:14:39.130
and also in the Ida B.
Wells, Southern Horrors,

01:14:39.130 --> 01:14:41.490
with an issue that I see when I write

01:14:41.490 --> 01:14:44.750
about colonialism, and
actually about Nazi crime,

01:14:44.750 --> 01:14:49.750
which is this notion that
we as a people are so legal,

01:14:50.650 --> 01:14:53.920
we are such people of
the law that we can tell

01:14:53.920 --> 01:14:55.720
when it's okay to make an exception.

01:14:57.439 --> 01:15:01.070
We are, by definition,
such legally-minded people

01:15:01.070 --> 01:15:06.070
that if we carry out a
lynching, ipso facto,

01:15:06.570 --> 01:15:08.710
that must have been justified,

01:15:08.710 --> 01:15:11.220
that state of exception
must have been justified.

01:15:11.220 --> 01:15:13.830
And you see that you
see that state of mind

01:15:15.460 --> 01:15:17.390
with the Germans in Eastern Europe.

01:15:17.390 --> 01:15:20.750
It's very similar, this
notion that we are obviously

01:15:20.750 --> 01:15:23.400
of a higher legal status
and the people we confront,

01:15:23.400 --> 01:15:25.260
and because of our legal knowledge,

01:15:25.260 --> 01:15:27.840
we know that it's okay
to make an exception.

01:15:27.840 --> 01:15:29.340
- [Khalil] That's right.

01:15:29.340 --> 01:15:31.040
- Because we're such legal people,

01:15:31.040 --> 01:15:33.960
we know which people
are outside of the law,

01:15:33.960 --> 01:15:35.860
we know when we can make an exception.

01:15:36.700 --> 01:15:38.020
- Can I just echo that?

01:15:38.020 --> 01:15:42.860
I wanted to make that point
in the preamble to the lecture

01:15:42.860 --> 01:15:45.600
about the foundational
lawlessness of the law itself,

01:15:45.600 --> 01:15:49.350
and to really nail the point
of a white outlaw culture

01:15:49.350 --> 01:15:53.213
embedded within a legal culture,
that on the face of things,

01:15:58.000 --> 01:16:00.860
possible to square these contradictions,

01:16:00.860 --> 01:16:03.240
other than the way you just described.

01:16:03.240 --> 01:16:06.680
And perhaps the more controversial analogy

01:16:06.680 --> 01:16:10.930
would be that white collar
crime and financial crimes

01:16:10.930 --> 01:16:15.110
in a culture that we have
today is very much about

01:16:15.110 --> 01:16:18.373
rewarding people who can break
the law and get away with it.

01:16:19.480 --> 01:16:21.300
And there's a kind of
culture of admiration

01:16:21.300 --> 01:16:23.310
and respect for it, because
if everyone's playing

01:16:23.310 --> 01:16:24.470
by the rules, then no one's gonna

01:16:24.470 --> 01:16:25.690
make any money, because the market

01:16:25.690 --> 01:16:28.480
is going to adjust perfectly
to perfect knowledge.

01:16:28.480 --> 01:16:30.250
We all know the same thing, then we're

01:16:30.250 --> 01:16:32.270
all competing at the same level.

01:16:32.270 --> 01:16:34.310
But it's precisely the moment at which I

01:16:34.310 --> 01:16:38.550
can exploit information,
which is often illegal

01:16:38.550 --> 01:16:41.810
to exploit that information,
that I can get make money,

01:16:41.810 --> 01:16:44.080
and maybe we catch these
people, maybe we don't.

01:16:44.080 --> 01:16:47.850
I remember hearing a survey
of self-reported crimes

01:16:47.850 --> 01:16:50.610
by financial sector
elite saying, basically,

01:16:50.610 --> 01:16:54.920
have you witnessed or know
of a crime being committed

01:16:54.920 --> 01:16:56.660
within the context of your work?

01:16:56.660 --> 01:16:59.027
And I'm just going to guess
what I heard at that moment,

01:16:59.027 --> 01:17:00.790
and people can look it up, but it was

01:17:00.790 --> 01:17:03.560
a large number, it was well over 50% said,

01:17:03.560 --> 01:17:05.850
yes, we've seen this all the time.

01:17:05.850 --> 01:17:07.050
And that's just the point, right?

01:17:07.050 --> 01:17:08.410
The rules are made to be broken

01:17:08.410 --> 01:17:11.440
by powerful people only
to reinforce that they're

01:17:11.440 --> 01:17:13.690
the right people to be
in control of the law.

01:17:16.713 --> 01:17:17.853
- Excellent.

01:17:20.380 --> 01:17:23.790
So, we've got a bunch of questions.

01:17:23.790 --> 01:17:28.790
One of them feeds right
into the Muhammad worldview,

01:17:29.100 --> 01:17:30.910
and one of them is the question,

01:17:30.910 --> 01:17:32.730
the main critique you always get.

01:17:32.730 --> 01:17:34.460
Which one do you want to start with?

01:17:34.460 --> 01:17:35.960
- Let's start with the critique.

01:17:35.960 --> 01:17:38.110
- Okay, so one of our students took

01:17:38.110 --> 01:17:40.053
Professor Phillip Goff's course,

01:17:44.730 --> 01:17:47.853
and of course, Phil, Professor Goff,

01:17:48.700 --> 01:17:51.002
statistics are going to be used.

01:17:51.002 --> 01:17:52.670
(Jason chuckling)

01:17:52.670 --> 01:17:54.843
So, this student wants to say,

01:17:58.179 --> 01:17:59.750
have you ever heard this question before?

01:17:59.750 --> 01:18:01.540
How do we grapple with the tension between

01:18:01.540 --> 01:18:04.690
simply knowing racism as
a truth of our existence,

01:18:04.690 --> 01:18:08.520
versus using statistics as
a way to prove our reality

01:18:08.520 --> 01:18:12.410
to those who don't experience
it, that is white people?

01:18:12.410 --> 01:18:15.800
- Yeah, so I think this
is a question saying,

01:18:15.800 --> 01:18:18.250
do we have to throw the baby
out with the bath water?

01:18:18.250 --> 01:18:19.277
- Right.
- Right.

01:18:20.650 --> 01:18:22.580
- So, you know, I don't have a good answer

01:18:22.580 --> 01:18:24.020
for this question any more than I had

01:18:24.020 --> 01:18:26.353
a decade ago when I first wrote this book.

01:18:27.330 --> 01:18:29.680
What I can tell you is that other people

01:18:29.680 --> 01:18:33.870
who study comparative
societies that are rooted

01:18:33.870 --> 01:18:37.280
in settler colonialism or colonialism,

01:18:37.280 --> 01:18:42.280
South Africa, Brazil, France,
essentially tell stories

01:18:43.720 --> 01:18:47.690
or have shown that in societies

01:18:47.690 --> 01:18:51.940
where there is little administrative

01:18:51.940 --> 01:18:55.260
or empirical data to
trace racial disparities,

01:18:55.260 --> 01:18:57.160
communities have had a harder time,

01:18:57.160 --> 01:18:59.470
who are the victims of systemic racism,

01:18:59.470 --> 01:19:03.130
in making the case for those experiences.

01:19:03.130 --> 01:19:08.130
And therefore, one could
argue that the other side

01:19:10.090 --> 01:19:12.390
of the coin of racial criminalization,

01:19:12.390 --> 01:19:14.940
vis-a-vis crime statistics, has been

01:19:14.940 --> 01:19:19.720
robust social movement activism
in defense of black people's

01:19:19.720 --> 01:19:23.440
civil and human rights, essentially using

01:19:23.440 --> 01:19:27.360
the same evidence
against their oppressors.

01:19:27.360 --> 01:19:31.370
And I don't know that I
can solve for that history

01:19:31.370 --> 01:19:34.343
any more by acknowledging
that contradiction.

01:19:35.500 --> 01:19:38.710
But if the question is,
how do we move forward,

01:19:38.710 --> 01:19:41.930
it seems to me that that's less an answer

01:19:41.930 --> 01:19:45.070
about whether we get rid
of racial crime statistics

01:19:45.070 --> 01:19:48.080
as some kind of early warning system,

01:19:48.080 --> 01:19:49.940
(Khalil chuckling)

01:19:49.940 --> 01:19:54.200
and get to re-socialize ourselves

01:19:54.200 --> 01:19:57.790
to a more universal understanding

01:19:57.790 --> 01:20:00.963
of the very outlaw culture
that I've just described.

01:20:02.080 --> 01:20:06.470
To borrow Tim's work here,
in a country that has used

01:20:06.470 --> 01:20:10.630
forms of memory laws to
prohibit deep socialization

01:20:10.630 --> 01:20:13.300
and learning about the
things that we are supposedly

01:20:13.300 --> 01:20:15.500
never to forget, we keep forgetting

01:20:15.500 --> 01:20:17.380
the same histories over and over again

01:20:17.380 --> 01:20:19.980
and producing similar
outcomes in our society.

01:20:19.980 --> 01:20:23.550
That, to me, seems to
be a first order problem

01:20:23.550 --> 01:20:27.660
before we can decide
whether or not using race

01:20:27.660 --> 01:20:29.730
as a way of measuring social phenomenon

01:20:29.730 --> 01:20:31.683
is no longer useful in our society.

01:20:33.550 --> 01:20:37.010
- Good, so okay, now let's flip,

01:20:37.010 --> 01:20:40.543
continuing on the theme of objectivity.

01:20:42.130 --> 01:20:44.970
So, here's a question on
the misuse and weaponization

01:20:44.970 --> 01:20:46.763
of objectivity in modern America.

01:20:50.922 --> 01:20:53.020
This is another, again,
a student in our class.

01:20:53.020 --> 01:20:56.998
We have a mixed audience
of public and students.

01:20:56.998 --> 01:21:01.300
So here, the question is,
how do you think crime data

01:21:01.300 --> 01:21:04.210
is weaponized and debates
about policing in major cities,

01:21:04.210 --> 01:21:07.020
especially with regard to
increased use of algorithms

01:21:07.020 --> 01:21:09.900
and widespread use of
surveillance technology?

01:21:09.900 --> 01:21:14.000
Okay, that's a bit of a
softball down the plate.

01:21:14.000 --> 01:21:17.680
- Yeah, no, it's a great question,
and I wrote a new preface

01:21:17.680 --> 01:21:21.420
to Condemnation two years
ago, and I had thought I

01:21:21.420 --> 01:21:23.100
might read a little bit
from it, but I didn't.

01:21:23.100 --> 01:21:26.510
But essentially, a couple of
years ago by way of anecdote,

01:21:26.510 --> 01:21:29.210
Bill Bratton announced CompStat 2.0

01:21:29.210 --> 01:21:30.767
at a conference that I was
attending in New York City.

01:21:30.767 --> 01:21:32.960
It was a Future of Cities Conference

01:21:32.960 --> 01:21:35.180
hosted by the Rockefeller Foundation,

01:21:35.180 --> 01:21:39.200
and he bragged about CompStat 2.0 being

01:21:39.200 --> 01:21:41.840
more predictive and not prejudicial,

01:21:41.840 --> 01:21:44.410
more discriminating
and not discriminatory,

01:21:44.410 --> 01:21:49.130
because this new algorithm
of hotspot policing

01:21:49.130 --> 01:21:51.600
was gonna pinpoint exactly
the right criminals

01:21:51.600 --> 01:21:53.190
in the community, sweep them away,

01:21:53.190 --> 01:21:54.790
and make communities safe again.

01:21:55.750 --> 01:21:58.840
And so, he even jokingly
said something to the effect

01:21:58.840 --> 01:22:02.360
that it was like Minority
Report, the Tom Cruise movie,

01:22:02.360 --> 01:22:05.830
where these precogs,
short for pre-cognition,

01:22:05.830 --> 01:22:08.100
could predict the future,
see a crime happen, and then,

01:22:08.100 --> 01:22:11.240
they'd send the police out,
Tom Cruise, for example,

01:22:11.240 --> 01:22:13.310
to stop the murder before it happened.

01:22:13.310 --> 01:22:16.780
And so, I write in the
preface that this notion

01:22:16.780 --> 01:22:20.530
of a Minority Report as a
film is a double entendre

01:22:20.530 --> 01:22:22.220
on the notion of a Minority Report

01:22:22.220 --> 01:22:24.880
focusing on black and brown people.

01:22:24.880 --> 01:22:27.770
So, to answer to your question, for much

01:22:27.770 --> 01:22:30.700
of policing today remains committed

01:22:30.700 --> 01:22:34.750
to the hyper-surveillance
of black communities,

01:22:34.750 --> 01:22:38.560
predicated on the notion
that the statistics show

01:22:38.560 --> 01:22:40.960
that there's lots of crime
in those communities,

01:22:40.960 --> 01:22:45.580
and therefore policing ought
to remain at the levels

01:22:45.580 --> 01:22:48.740
that it has historically
been and ought to be

01:22:48.740 --> 01:22:52.430
the primary instrument for
delivering public safety.

01:22:52.430 --> 01:22:55.110
Now, you didn't read this in the book,

01:22:55.110 --> 01:22:58.680
and I talked a bit about it in the lecture

01:22:58.680 --> 01:23:01.990
for this obvious reason, that if we

01:23:01.990 --> 01:23:04.700
are to take history,
potentially, as some guide

01:23:05.820 --> 01:23:08.620
to what might be possible
in the present, and people

01:23:08.620 --> 01:23:11.597
can disagree about whether
that can happen or not,

01:23:11.597 --> 01:23:13.820
policing was never the solution to all

01:23:13.820 --> 01:23:15.810
the antisocial behavior
in the white community.

01:23:15.810 --> 01:23:19.700
It's not now, and it wasn't
then after we got through

01:23:19.700 --> 01:23:21.680
with the eugenics period and the period

01:23:21.680 --> 01:23:24.380
of nativist reaction to immigrants.

01:23:24.380 --> 01:23:26.913
So, the response was social investment.

01:23:27.820 --> 01:23:31.980
So, we seem to be stuck on
allowing law enforcement

01:23:31.980 --> 01:23:35.000
to define its bureaucratic imperatives

01:23:35.000 --> 01:23:38.950
as our moral imperatives, and therefore,

01:23:38.950 --> 01:23:42.030
it's going to use its
evidence as best it can

01:23:42.030 --> 01:23:45.540
to make its argument, which
is to continue to point

01:23:45.540 --> 01:23:48.240
to crime statistics as the proof.

01:23:48.240 --> 01:23:52.410
But aside from everything
we might say to complicate

01:23:52.410 --> 01:23:54.920
what does a crime actually
mean in the social context?

01:23:54.920 --> 01:23:59.070
To use Tommy Shelby's work,
my colleague who makes

01:23:59.070 --> 01:24:02.000
a very sophisticated argument
that as a philosopher,

01:24:02.000 --> 01:24:05.120
essentially says, we can't
judge the crime in the community

01:24:05.120 --> 01:24:07.420
if we can't deal with
the crimes of the state.

01:24:08.490 --> 01:24:10.870
And so anyway, we could go down

01:24:10.870 --> 01:24:12.690
the road there, but that's my reaction.

01:24:12.690 --> 01:24:17.683
- Yeah, just an aside.
it's just so incredibly,

01:24:19.250 --> 01:24:21.760
frustrating under-sells it to see

01:24:21.760 --> 01:24:23.770
this repeat itself again and again.

01:24:23.770 --> 01:24:26.683
Professor Hinton's first
book is on this topic,

01:24:28.100 --> 01:24:31.040
she's speaking later in the
seminar, we're reading both

01:24:31.040 --> 01:24:34.720
of her books, the removal of social aid

01:24:34.720 --> 01:24:37.140
and support and just criminal solutions.

01:24:37.140 --> 01:24:39.200
And again, and again, and again.

01:24:39.200 --> 01:24:40.250
- [Khalil] And again.

01:24:41.670 --> 01:24:45.332
- I won't ask you what people
ask me: what's the solution?

01:24:45.332 --> 01:24:48.165
(Khalil laughing)

01:24:50.350 --> 01:24:51.183
- All right.

01:24:58.598 --> 01:25:00.348
So, another question.

01:25:01.970 --> 01:25:04.160
One of the audience members is curious

01:25:04.160 --> 01:25:06.790
about Hoffman's legacy,
in terms of the emphasis

01:25:07.771 --> 01:25:11.600
of law and order and the war
on crime some 60 years later.

01:25:11.600 --> 01:25:13.320
So, now we are getting into Hinton's

01:25:13.320 --> 01:25:14.790
work.
- Well,

01:25:14.790 --> 01:25:16.170
unless there's an add on to that question,

01:25:16.170 --> 01:25:17.390
there is an interesting footnote

01:25:17.390 --> 01:25:19.710
that isn't an obvious from what they read.

01:25:19.710 --> 01:25:20.580
- Oh, go on.

01:25:20.580 --> 01:25:23.070
- Yeah, so very quickly, Hoffman

01:25:23.070 --> 01:25:25.710
couldn't be more interesting as a writer.

01:25:25.710 --> 01:25:29.700
So, it might've been good
enough to read this book,

01:25:29.700 --> 01:25:31.377
to put it in this historical context

01:25:31.377 --> 01:25:33.470
and to see his contribution to the way

01:25:33.470 --> 01:25:35.320
that we have this conversation today.

01:25:36.210 --> 01:25:37.960
That's the point of chapter two.

01:25:37.960 --> 01:25:41.870
But it turns out that Hoffman
is such a seminal figure

01:25:41.870 --> 01:25:45.180
that his study of homicide in the country

01:25:45.180 --> 01:25:48.380
makes him a sole source authority

01:25:48.380 --> 01:25:52.333
on national homicide
statistics until the 1920s.

01:25:53.170 --> 01:25:55.533
It really is until the federal,

01:25:59.540 --> 01:26:03.100
the need for nationalizing
the collection of crime data

01:26:03.100 --> 01:26:07.840
that Hoffman is the number
one source for making claims

01:26:07.840 --> 01:26:10.757
about who's dying where
in what parts of the city.

01:26:10.757 --> 01:26:15.430
And he's so credited for
it that he's award-winning,

01:26:15.430 --> 01:26:17.740
so on and so forth, but one quick note.

01:26:17.740 --> 01:26:21.080
His longevity is so
significant, and his place

01:26:21.080 --> 01:26:24.750
in this sort of social
economy is so significant

01:26:24.750 --> 01:26:28.490
that the National Urban League,
a civil rights organization,

01:26:28.490 --> 01:26:32.300
invites him to a conference
in the 1920s to basically give

01:26:32.300 --> 01:26:35.690
the statistics on homicide in
black America, because if you

01:26:35.690 --> 01:26:39.071
want to know who's dying
and of what, he's the guy.

01:26:39.071 --> 01:26:39.904
(Khalil chuckling)

01:26:39.904 --> 01:26:43.590
And Du Bois is either
there or gets wind of it,

01:26:43.590 --> 01:26:46.340
and he proverbially pulls his hair out,

01:26:46.340 --> 01:26:48.910
because he's like this guy won't

01:26:48.910 --> 01:26:52.280
go away, I cannot get rid of him.

01:26:52.280 --> 01:26:54.280
So, to the questioner's point,

01:26:54.280 --> 01:26:57.580
Hoffman lasts much longer than the 1890s

01:26:57.580 --> 01:27:00.330
when this book is published
in his significance.

01:27:00.330 --> 01:27:03.040
- So, I noticed in Aldean Morris's book,

01:27:03.040 --> 01:27:06.860
A Scholar Denied, when he's
describing Dubois' conferences

01:27:06.860 --> 01:27:09.660
in the Atlantic, they looked a lot like

01:27:09.660 --> 01:27:12.712
year by year responses to Hoffman's book.

01:27:12.712 --> 01:27:13.800
(Khalil laughing)

01:27:13.800 --> 01:27:15.380
Is that right?

01:27:15.380 --> 01:27:17.540
- I don't know--
- One is on disease,

01:27:17.540 --> 01:27:19.510
one is on--
- Yeah.

01:27:19.510 --> 01:27:24.510
I would say that a lot of academics

01:27:24.790 --> 01:27:29.406
build their careers frustrated
with some body of work

01:27:29.406 --> 01:27:30.847
(Jason laughing)

01:27:30.847 --> 01:27:32.550
that they want to take down.

01:27:32.550 --> 01:27:35.680
And I don't think it's too
much of a stretch to say

01:27:35.680 --> 01:27:39.020
that Hoffman was a
consummate foil for Du Bois.

01:27:39.020 --> 01:27:40.343
- Right, makes sense.

01:27:46.580 --> 01:27:49.580
Right, but to continue the question,

01:27:50.570 --> 01:27:55.570
the person wanted you to draw
parallels between the 1960s,

01:28:01.709 --> 01:28:03.220
the Frontlash era, as it were,

01:28:03.220 --> 01:28:07.223
to borrow Vesla Weaver's
terminology, and Hoffman.

01:28:08.970 --> 01:28:10.520
So, how do you, yeah.

01:28:10.520 --> 01:28:12.400
- Yeah, well, I'll just say,

01:28:12.400 --> 01:28:15.920
and I'm gonna draw my
former IU colleague's work,

01:28:15.920 --> 01:28:20.040
Nicole Siegel, who's written
a book called Violence Work.

01:28:20.040 --> 01:28:22.960
And what's fascinating
about that work, a rich

01:28:22.960 --> 01:28:27.960
archival study, is to look
explicitly at the relationship

01:28:28.620 --> 01:28:33.090
between the academic
community of criminologists

01:28:33.090 --> 01:28:38.090
and the clinical practice
of law enforcement,

01:28:38.290 --> 01:28:39.950
and that these two things bleed

01:28:39.950 --> 01:28:43.040
into each other beginning in the 1960s.

01:28:43.040 --> 01:28:47.580
And so, there's a kind of
technocratic consensus between

01:28:47.580 --> 01:28:52.580
a body of academic research
and basic law enforcement

01:28:53.010 --> 01:28:58.010
that there's no room for
any critical distance.

01:28:58.210 --> 01:29:01.490
Essentially, the academic
community is a handmaiden

01:29:01.490 --> 01:29:03.440
to the law enforcement's need.

01:29:03.440 --> 01:29:07.160
They need them to tell them,
how can we do our jobs better,

01:29:07.160 --> 01:29:09.970
how can we be more effective at this work?

01:29:09.970 --> 01:29:14.370
And while this might not
be an entirely a surprise,

01:29:14.370 --> 01:29:17.190
it neutralizes and mitigates the kinds

01:29:17.190 --> 01:29:19.920
of critiques that come from other

01:29:19.920 --> 01:29:23.540
academic pockets and
other kinds of research,

01:29:23.540 --> 01:29:27.080
such that law enforcement
is not accountable.

01:29:27.080 --> 01:29:29.900
They can point to the
very research community

01:29:29.900 --> 01:29:33.050
that they are both getting funding from,

01:29:33.050 --> 01:29:35.580
from federal sources
increasingly, to do this work.

01:29:35.580 --> 01:29:39.660
And so, everybody, in
the way that Hoffman is,

01:29:39.660 --> 01:29:44.140
can argue for empiricism as the currency

01:29:44.140 --> 01:29:49.140
for policymaking that is
fundamentally discriminatory

01:29:49.170 --> 01:29:54.170
and is causing so much social
activism, that, of course,

01:29:54.990 --> 01:29:57.290
Elizabeth is writing
about in her second book.

01:30:00.450 --> 01:30:04.393
- So, here's the next question,
also from a seminar member.

01:30:06.160 --> 01:30:07.820
Given the perception that, quote,

01:30:07.820 --> 01:30:11.450
education makes the black
person a criminal, unquote,

01:30:11.450 --> 01:30:14.110
how can we understand the
process of reformation

01:30:14.110 --> 01:30:16.813
within the 19th and 20th
Century prison systems?

01:30:18.020 --> 01:30:19.350
We've been going chronologically,

01:30:19.350 --> 01:30:21.540
because it's a history class,

01:30:21.540 --> 01:30:23.993
and that's apparently how you guys roll.

01:30:23.993 --> 01:30:27.060
(men chuckling)

01:30:27.060 --> 01:30:32.060
But so, we've read
Foucault, we started with

01:30:34.520 --> 01:30:39.253
Austin Reed's book, and we've
been looking at reformation.

01:30:40.770 --> 01:30:44.020
So, the question is how does,

01:30:44.020 --> 01:30:46.514
so, there's moral
reformation through religion

01:30:46.514 --> 01:30:49.403
and physical reformation through work,

01:30:50.410 --> 01:30:54.743
but how does this discussion of education

01:30:56.810 --> 01:31:00.930
increases criminality play
into prison reformation,

01:31:00.930 --> 01:31:03.120
discussion about intellectual reformation,

01:31:03.120 --> 01:31:05.143
in the late 19th and early 20th Century?

01:31:06.245 --> 01:31:08.560
I guess to build on the question is,

01:31:08.560 --> 01:31:11.630
is the result that the
reformation discussions

01:31:13.719 --> 01:31:15.940
in prisons in late
19th, early 20th Century

01:31:15.940 --> 01:31:19.520
exclude teaching, exclude prison?

01:31:19.520 --> 01:31:21.170
I mean, it's really interesting.

01:31:21.170 --> 01:31:23.720
I'm tempted to go to the
areas I know in the 90s

01:31:23.720 --> 01:31:28.353
and removing prison education,
which happened in the 90s,

01:31:30.819 --> 01:31:35.819
as it, education was not
important to reformation at all.

01:31:36.600 --> 01:31:40.303
But is that a theme that, in
late 19th, early 20th Century,

01:31:42.190 --> 01:31:45.450
was educational intellectual reformation

01:31:46.900 --> 01:31:49.180
frowned upon as a result
of these discussions?

01:31:49.180 --> 01:31:51.720
- So, let me cut to the first argument,

01:31:51.720 --> 01:31:53.270
or the first a bit of evidence,

01:31:53.270 --> 01:31:54.810
and then back into that question.

01:31:54.810 --> 01:31:59.040
So first of all, what is
happening in the context

01:31:59.040 --> 01:32:03.290
of reformation does not
apply to the vast majority

01:32:03.290 --> 01:32:05.510
of African-Americans who
are still southerners

01:32:05.510 --> 01:32:08.583
in the late 19th and early 20th Century.

01:32:10.310 --> 01:32:12.910
Prison scholars, including
Rebecca McLennan and others,

01:32:12.910 --> 01:32:17.910
see the broader period,
say between 1870 and 1920,

01:32:18.400 --> 01:32:22.190
as a progressive project,
in Rebecca McClennan's words

01:32:22.190 --> 01:32:24.850
as a kind of effort at manly citizenship,

01:32:24.850 --> 01:32:27.560
but it is thoroughly
racialized in the direction

01:32:27.560 --> 01:32:30.590
of what is required to give white men

01:32:30.590 --> 01:32:35.200
a second chance through the
incarceration experience.

01:32:35.200 --> 01:32:38.060
Mostly, that is about
work, so there is still

01:32:38.060 --> 01:32:41.550
a carceral regime of extraction from men

01:32:41.550 --> 01:32:44.780
in the name of rehabilitation.

01:32:44.780 --> 01:32:46.960
But increasingly they get
the kind of programming

01:32:46.960 --> 01:32:49.280
that we would recognize
in more modern times.

01:32:49.280 --> 01:32:53.340
They get to publish newspapers,
they get, various forms

01:32:53.340 --> 01:32:55.693
of arts and entertainment,
theater productions.

01:32:56.720 --> 01:33:00.090
There isn't formal
education that I'm aware of,

01:33:00.090 --> 01:33:02.160
and I've studied this period pretty well,

01:33:02.160 --> 01:33:05.450
in a way of people
getting GEDs, for example.

01:33:05.450 --> 01:33:07.960
But in anything's possible,
and so I don't want to exclude

01:33:07.960 --> 01:33:10.350
that just because I can't
recall that being part of it.

01:33:10.350 --> 01:33:13.320
So, there is a kind of a series

01:33:14.240 --> 01:33:17.530
of intellectual nourishment
that comes through the arts

01:33:17.530 --> 01:33:20.390
in other kinds of writing
forums, for people

01:33:20.390 --> 01:33:23.190
who are publishing
newsletters in these places.

01:33:23.190 --> 01:33:28.190
But these are places that
house 80% white populations.

01:33:28.470 --> 01:33:31.870
There are small numbers of incarcerated

01:33:31.870 --> 01:33:33.650
people of color in Chicago, New York,

01:33:33.650 --> 01:33:37.433
Philadelphia, Los Angeles,
in these early days.

01:33:38.390 --> 01:33:41.130
Maybe not so much Los
Angeles, let me hold off

01:33:41.130 --> 01:33:43.740
on that precisely because of Kelly's work.

01:33:43.740 --> 01:33:44.920
But in the places that I've looked

01:33:44.920 --> 01:33:47.400
at these histories,
that's what it looks like.

01:33:47.400 --> 01:33:50.840
It's the South that is
literally the Dark Ages,

01:33:50.840 --> 01:33:54.000
and there's no effort to educate anybody.

01:33:54.000 --> 01:33:56.750
So, what I point to in the presentation

01:33:56.750 --> 01:33:59.740
and what you heard has everything to do

01:33:59.740 --> 01:34:03.510
with the larger
justification for, at best,

01:34:03.510 --> 01:34:06.070
a sharecropper's education in the South,

01:34:06.070 --> 01:34:08.810
because too much education
would give people the tools

01:34:08.810 --> 01:34:12.640
to question society,
and therefore that was

01:34:12.640 --> 01:34:17.610
not consistent with the
racial project of control.

01:34:17.610 --> 01:34:18.443
- Right.

01:34:18.443 --> 01:34:22.460
It's kind of truly remarkable
when we think about

01:34:22.460 --> 01:34:25.210
what happened in nine in the 90s,

01:34:25.210 --> 01:34:29.453
when they removed funding
for prison education.

01:34:30.460 --> 01:34:32.513
That that was the 1990s.

01:34:33.550 --> 01:34:34.910
- That that was austerity.

01:34:34.910 --> 01:34:37.720
So that was a version of neo-liberalism

01:34:39.420 --> 01:34:43.793
pointing to a punitiveness
as a public good,

01:34:44.700 --> 01:34:48.463
without encouraging forms of dependency,

01:34:51.803 --> 01:34:53.770
and that is for the state.

01:34:53.770 --> 01:34:56.790
And so, you could invest
in more punishment

01:34:57.730 --> 01:35:00.960
as a form of incapacitation,
and maybe the logic

01:35:00.960 --> 01:35:04.140
of deterrence, without making good

01:35:04.140 --> 01:35:07.990
on any other possibilities,
like, pulling Pell grants,

01:35:07.990 --> 01:35:10.853
pulling programming for education inside.

01:35:12.390 --> 01:35:15.360
- Okay, so, maybe we have
time for one more question.

01:35:15.360 --> 01:35:16.193
Let's see.

01:35:23.108 --> 01:35:24.410
So, we've a couple more questions

01:35:24.410 --> 01:35:26.403
on black education, but I think we've,

01:35:32.730 --> 01:35:34.580
Let me see what question to close on.

01:35:36.230 --> 01:35:39.870
Well, do you want to
talk about the legacy,

01:35:39.870 --> 01:35:42.270
maybe tying some of these themes together,

01:35:42.270 --> 01:35:44.533
I'm going to just jealously
ask you to end on this,

01:35:44.533 --> 01:35:49.043
because I'm really interested
in this whole idea.

01:35:55.484 --> 01:35:59.067
I'm interested in the
modern sort of racism

01:36:02.540 --> 01:36:06.370
this stereotype that black
people, black Americans,

01:36:06.370 --> 01:36:08.830
are anti-education, or view education

01:36:11.105 --> 01:36:14.870
as white has been disturbing me of late.

01:36:14.870 --> 01:36:19.260
So the question was, how does
this legacy of criminality

01:36:19.260 --> 01:36:23.010
and education fit into, have a bearing,

01:36:23.010 --> 01:36:28.010
on contemporary discussions
of black people on education?

01:36:28.360 --> 01:36:31.170
- Yeah, well, there are a lot of them,

01:36:31.170 --> 01:36:33.380
but there's one I skipped
over in the presentation.

01:36:33.380 --> 01:36:35.963
So, in the interest of efficiency,

01:36:36.900 --> 01:36:39.773
which of course is a major
theme in these arguments.

01:36:40.810 --> 01:36:42.330
I'm going to show you
something I skipped over,

01:36:42.330 --> 01:36:45.270
because I think it gets
at a little bit of this.

01:36:45.270 --> 01:36:50.270
So, there's a section of the
book, it's not in the chapter

01:36:50.820 --> 01:36:55.440
the students read, where
part of the rationale,

01:36:55.440 --> 01:37:00.013
I mean, part of the emphasis
of the book and these echoes

01:37:01.090 --> 01:37:04.960
is that so much of what
we might easily dismiss

01:37:04.960 --> 01:37:09.200
as white supremacy required so much effort

01:37:10.140 --> 01:37:12.293
at a performance of objectivity.

01:37:13.480 --> 01:37:15.380
People wanted to be taken seriously,

01:37:15.380 --> 01:37:19.360
people wanted not to
be dismissed as racist.

01:37:19.360 --> 01:37:22.680
And that tendency today to
turn to empirical evidence

01:37:22.680 --> 01:37:24.850
to end an argument or resolve a debate

01:37:24.850 --> 01:37:27.670
is the same tendency to
say, I'm not a racist,

01:37:27.670 --> 01:37:29.100
black people commit more crime

01:37:29.100 --> 01:37:30.893
than anybody else, it's a fact.

01:37:32.380 --> 01:37:35.240
That's the structure of the debate.

01:37:35.240 --> 01:37:36.990
And so, part of the
structure of the debate

01:37:36.990 --> 01:37:40.490
that we carry with us is that
people having this debate

01:37:40.490 --> 01:37:43.990
about how to explain these
disparities a hundred years ago

01:37:45.290 --> 01:37:48.720
was to then say, okay,
well, maybe we should look,

01:37:48.720 --> 01:37:52.210
if education really
works, if education really

01:37:53.420 --> 01:37:55.970
is just about opportunity
and we just need to extend

01:37:55.970 --> 01:37:59.047
more opportunity, and
that will be the answer

01:37:59.047 --> 01:38:02.850
to these disparities, I'm
talking a hundred years ago,

01:38:02.850 --> 01:38:05.980
then people started looking
at the criminal records

01:38:05.980 --> 01:38:09.633
of the alumni of various schools, HBCUs.

01:38:10.570 --> 01:38:12.960
Theodore Roosevelt did it
at Hampton and Tuskegee,

01:38:12.960 --> 01:38:14.610
as you know, Jason, from having

01:38:14.610 --> 01:38:17.293
read the book, and it's remarkable.

01:38:18.630 --> 01:38:20.100
Booker T. Washington
writes an article about it,

01:38:20.100 --> 01:38:23.130
and he's basically saying,
you can count the alumni

01:38:23.130 --> 01:38:25.570
of HBCUs and it's just a handful

01:38:25.570 --> 01:38:27.683
can be counted in criminal records.

01:38:29.050 --> 01:38:33.180
And what's interesting
is there's no equivalency

01:38:33.180 --> 01:38:37.170
that I ever found amongst
various immigrant populations

01:38:37.170 --> 01:38:39.110
that had shared this similar container

01:38:39.110 --> 01:38:40.683
of criminalization for a time.

01:38:41.610 --> 01:38:43.640
In fact, I'm not the only
one who pointed it out.

01:38:43.640 --> 01:38:44.900
A guy named Ray Stannard Baker,

01:38:44.900 --> 01:38:46.820
who was a liberal progressive at a time,

01:38:46.820 --> 01:38:48.890
they wrote a book called
Along the Color Line,

01:38:48.890 --> 01:38:51.210
and this was probably one of
the more progressive books

01:38:51.210 --> 01:38:53.270
that could point to a
lot of contradictions.

01:38:53.270 --> 01:38:55.350
It's cited in Condemnation.

01:38:55.350 --> 01:38:59.040
And he pointed out, he
said, look, we keep counting

01:39:00.190 --> 01:39:03.860
what happens to black
people in these schools

01:39:03.860 --> 01:39:08.400
as some kind of evidence of
whether or not the school itself

01:39:08.400 --> 01:39:12.680
is important or useful,
when we don't do the same

01:39:12.680 --> 01:39:15.740
for places like Harvard and other places.

01:39:15.740 --> 01:39:18.860
And his point is that if
we started to keep track

01:39:18.860 --> 01:39:21.390
of the criminality coming out of

01:39:21.390 --> 01:39:23.960
mainstream, elite white institutions,

01:39:23.960 --> 01:39:26.920
and if we did an economic
analysis of it, there would be

01:39:26.920 --> 01:39:31.600
untold damage done to individuals
and to society by virtue

01:39:31.600 --> 01:39:36.223
of the alumni behavior
coming out of these schools.

01:39:39.393 --> 01:39:44.393
Every financial crime that has
nearly destroyed the economy

01:39:44.870 --> 01:39:48.897
on a scale of damage to
individual lives is incalculable

01:39:48.897 --> 01:39:50.960
relative to someone who's a victim

01:39:50.960 --> 01:39:54.330
of pick-pocketing,
burglary, or something else.

01:39:54.330 --> 01:39:57.290
And by the way, homicide is a tiny part

01:39:57.290 --> 01:40:00.730
of criminal offenses,
about 5%, violent crime.

01:40:00.730 --> 01:40:03.470
So, most of what is crime and causes us

01:40:03.470 --> 01:40:07.610
to have mass incarceration
is not killing somebody,

01:40:07.610 --> 01:40:10.210
but something else, and
often something quite petty.

01:40:11.290 --> 01:40:16.020
So, I point out that that economists today

01:40:16.020 --> 01:40:17.510
are still using that framework.

01:40:17.510 --> 01:40:20.660
They're still judging whether
a school is working or not

01:40:20.660 --> 01:40:23.910
based on the crime rates of
black kids who attend them.

01:40:23.910 --> 01:40:26.540
It's crazy, it is totally absurd,

01:40:26.540 --> 01:40:29.540
as if the value of the public good itself

01:40:29.540 --> 01:40:32.350
ought to be judged against whether or not

01:40:32.350 --> 01:40:35.190
people engage in criminal activity.

01:40:35.190 --> 01:40:38.340
Because what we
essentially are closing off

01:40:38.340 --> 01:40:43.340
is the possibility that there
is nothing but association

01:40:44.240 --> 01:40:48.610
and there is no causation,
and essentially the drivers

01:40:48.610 --> 01:40:51.070
of antisocial behavior and criminality

01:40:52.862 --> 01:40:56.970
are separate and apart from
whether or not kids are

01:41:01.440 --> 01:41:04.950
school experience, but this is a legacy.

01:41:04.950 --> 01:41:07.000
I won't give it all to Hoffman,

01:41:07.000 --> 01:41:09.300
but he most certainly
advanced the argument

01:41:09.300 --> 01:41:11.723
directly with regard to African-Americans.

01:41:13.010 --> 01:41:15.350
- So, on the note that Harvard, Yale,

01:41:15.350 --> 01:41:18.696
and Princeton lead directly to crime,

01:41:18.696 --> 01:41:22.350
(Jason chuckling)

01:41:22.350 --> 01:41:25.610
- Yeah, imagine a roster, I
mean, I'm not an economist,

01:41:25.610 --> 01:41:29.310
but you could just take
kind of the most celebrated

01:41:29.310 --> 01:41:32.150
white collar crime of the last 50 years

01:41:32.150 --> 01:41:37.150
actually, names, sentences, crime itself,

01:41:37.830 --> 01:41:41.500
and then do an estimate
on the actual damage

01:41:41.500 --> 01:41:44.100
to people's lives, including
people killing themselves

01:41:44.100 --> 01:41:47.773
because they lost a job,
or however it plays out,

01:41:47.773 --> 01:41:49.150
- [Jason] Or a house.

01:41:49.150 --> 01:41:51.120
- Or house, you name it.
- Or a mortgage,

01:41:51.120 --> 01:41:53.330
then all the violent crimes that resulted

01:41:53.330 --> 01:41:54.670
from that.
- Or domestic violence

01:41:54.670 --> 01:41:58.340
driven by economic challenges, which is,

01:41:58.340 --> 01:41:59.930
I'm not the social scientist, per se,

01:41:59.930 --> 01:42:01.970
but this is a major
argument of social science,

01:42:01.970 --> 01:42:04.420
that domestic violence
is often a consequence

01:42:04.420 --> 01:42:06.520
of the difficult economic realities

01:42:06.520 --> 01:42:08.653
that men, in particular, face.

01:42:09.680 --> 01:42:10.513
So, that's the point.

01:42:10.513 --> 01:42:12.970
The choice we make for the data we collect

01:42:12.970 --> 01:42:14.370
and the arguments we make about it

01:42:14.370 --> 01:42:16.663
is an expression of power itself.

01:42:17.550 --> 01:42:20.160
And so, hopefully our
students will learn that,

01:42:20.160 --> 01:42:22.427
and they will use their power wisely.

01:42:22.427 --> 01:42:23.790
(Khalil chuckling)

01:42:23.790 --> 01:42:26.410
- On that wonderful
note, thank you so much,

01:42:26.410 --> 01:42:29.900
Professor Muhammad,
Khalil, that was wonderful.

01:42:29.900 --> 01:42:31.615
Thank you for your vital work.

01:42:31.615 --> 01:42:33.390
- Thank you so much for having me.

01:42:33.390 --> 01:42:35.870
I hope to see everybody in person one day.

01:42:35.870 --> 01:42:37.640
- Yeah, we would like that.

01:42:37.640 --> 01:42:39.340
- Okay, bye-bye

01:42:39.340 --> 01:42:40.173
- Bye, everyone.

